9.1 to a dedicated §12.x SCP-self tooling section. Forward-compat; no deadline. Filed at TF-020P-005 R1 completeness COMP-MIN-001.\\nSTATUS.md:238:- **TF-020P-005c**: optional `SCP_PRE_PUSH_VERSION_STRICT=1` env var to make `scripts/scp-pre-push-verify.sh`'s `.tool-versions` parity check fatal — for CI-in-CI or strict local-dev workflows where version drift should hard-fail. Default behaviour stays non-fatal warning per slice 020P-005 design (DISPATCH-NOTE Risk #1). Closure path: opportunistic; file when a second requestor surfaces the need. Forward-compat; no deadline. Filed at TF-020P-005 R1 completeness COMP-NIT-003.\\nSTATUS.md:239:\\nSTATUS.md:240:## Tracked-forward items from WP-SCP-023 023A (plan-doc) + 023B (emitter) + 023C (aggregator)\\nSTATUS.md:241:\\nSTATUS.md:242:- **TF-023A-001** (low priority): R-023-01 k-anonymity mitigation framing clarification when WP-SCP-024 cascade slice begins. The plan-doc R-023-01 risk row references \\\"slice 024 introduces aggregator-side k-anonymity\\\"; tighten when WP-SCP-024 plan-doc lands. Forward-compat; opportunistic.\\nSTATUS.md:243:- **TF-023A-002** (medium priority): Cross-repo notification (v1.2.0 release announcement to ACC / CT / mapp-estate-regression) — **re-deferred to slice 023E** (was 023D; 023D ships exposure surface but Threshold A onboarding is where adopters first participate). Until adopters can opt in with the exposure surface live, the notification is a \\\"pre-Threshold-A FYI\\\" only. Filed at WP-SCP-023 023A; re-deferred at 023D R1 completeness CMP-MIN-001.\\nSTATUS.md:244:- **TF-023B-001** (low priority): Workflow-selftest harness `fixture-pass-with-scorecard-emit` — extend `tests/workflow/fixture-pass/` to cover the new opt-in `scorecard-emit: true` input on the SCP self-dogfood wrapper. Target slice 023C (when SCP self-dogfood wrapper is extended with `scorecard-emit: true` to feed the aggregator's first run). Forward-compat. Filed at 023B R1 completeness CG-MIN-005.\\nSTATUS.md:245:- **TF-023B-002** (low priority): FLA-pilot canary scorecard fixture — capture FLA's first successful scorecard emit at slice 023E as a permanent fixture. Target slice 023E. Filed at 023B R1 completeness CG-nit-003.\\nSTATUS.md:246:- **TF-023B-003** (low priority): Promote `WARN_BASELINE_RULES` to data-driven manifest per TF-020P-001's scope. Filed at 023B R3 R3-MIN-SAFE-002.\\nSTATUS.md:247:- **TF-023C-001** (medium priority): SCP self-dogfood — opt the SCP repo's own `policy-check-wrapper.yml` self-test into `scorecard-emit: true` so the aggregator has a 1-adopter test corpus before real adopters arrive. Target slice 023E (or earlier).\\nSTATUS.md:248:- **TF-023C-002** (low priority): cron-health monitor — alert when `scorecard-aggregator.yml` hasn't produced a fresh index in >2 weeks. Target slice 023E or post-Threshold-A maintenance.\\nSTATUS.md:249:- **TF-023C-003** (low priority): rate-limit handling for large estate. Filed at 023C R1 completeness CG-nit-002.\\nSTATUS.md:250:- **TF-023C-004** (low priority): aggregator job `timeout-minutes` constraint. Filed at 023C R1 safety nit-SAFE-004.\\nSTATUS.md:251:- **TF-023C-005** (forward-compat): SCP repo rename/transfer — update the regex anchor in `schemas/scorecard-opt-in-registry.schema.json` + `SCP_WORKFLOW_REF_PREFIX` constant. Filed at 023C R1 safety MAJ-SAFE-001 closure.\\nSTATUS.md:252:- **TF-023C-006** (low priority): Minimum `scp_version` check on verified emits. An adopter pinning a very old SCP version remains 'verified' since the aggregator is informational-not-authoritative (invariant 1); revisit if estate-wide drift becomes material. Filed at 023C R1 safety MIN-SAFE-002.\\nSTATUS.md:253:- **TF-023C-007** (low priority): Adopter wrapper filename constraint (must be `policy-check.yml`) — document at slice 023D ADOPT-001 §13 onboarding. Filed at 023C R1 completeness CG-MIN-006.\\nSTATUS.md:254:- **TF-023D-001** (low priority): `scp.audit_scorecard_changed` MCP method (write-side). Plan-doc §1 names this as forward-looking; 023D ships only the read-only `consult_scorecard`. Out of scope per invariant 5 (read-only); revisit only if a write surface emerges as necessary. Filed at 023D DISPATCH-NOTE forward-looking.\\nSTATUS.md:255:- **TF-023D-002** (low priority): markdown report retention policy — keep last 90 days; compact older. Defer until ~Q3 2026 (~13 weekly reports accumulated). Filed at 023D DISPATCH-NOTE forward-looking.\\nSTATUS.md:256:- **TF-023D-003** (medium priority): `_log_tool_invocation` carries `key_id=\\\"pending_021J\\\"` for every MCP tool — pre-existing audit-trail gap. consult_scorecard exposes the gap because it's the first MCP method exposing aggregated estate-wide data. Closure path: replace with active key_id from `docs/security/mcp-signing-keys.pub` before slice 023E / Threshold A sign-off. Filed at 023D R1 safety MAJ-SAFE-001.\\nSTATUS.md:257:- **TF-023D-004** (medium priority): drift section in markdown report — plan-doc §5 + DISPATCH-NOTE AC (i) named it. Deferred at 023D because real adopter data is needed to compute drift (until 023E onboards adopters, no drift to surface). Closure path: at slice 023E or first weekly run with ≥2 verified adopters, implement either prior-markdown-comparison or prior-index-comparison. Filed at 023D R1 completeness CMP-MAJ-001.\\nSTATUS.md:258:- **TF-023D-005** (low priority): MCP server redeploy — `scp.consult_scorecard` MCP method registration is a code change; the deployed MCP server at acc.brokapps.ai requires a redeploy to make the method callable by agents. Closure path: bundle with next ACC deployment cycle. Filed at 023D R1 completeness CMP-MIN-003.\\nSTATUS.md:259:- **TF-023E-001** (medium priority): SCP-self scorecard-emit opt-in is **deferred** until `standards-control-plane-` becomes public OR transfers to an org. GitHub blocks `actions/attest-build-provenance` on user-owned private repos with \\\"Feature not available for user-owned private repositories\\\" — D-042's trust model requires every emit to carry an OIDC attestation, and a self-exemption would weaken it. Surface impact: TF-023C-001 (1-adopter test corpus via SCP-self) cannot close at slice 023E; first real adopter must be public/org-owned (likely FLA, mapp-doc-agent, or an org-owned ACC/CT subrepo). Closure path: (a) make the SCP repo public, OR (b) transfer to an org, OR (c) accept SCP-self perpetually as `verification_failure` in the index (rejected at 023E fix-round-3 — degrades trust signal). Surfaced at 023E CI fix-round-3 (the first PR to set `scorecard-emit: true` on the wrapper failed at the attestation step with the GitHub error message). Cross-references USER-GATE-D criterion (i) carve-out.\\nSTATUS.md:260:- **TF-023E-002** (medium priority): SCP-self wrapper SHA pin is **stuck at v1.0.0 (@41a5299)** — bumping to any post-WP-SCP-023 SHA produces `startup_failure` because GitHub Actions validates declared permissions on ALL jobs in a called workflow at startup (BEFORE `if:` evaluation) against the caller's ceiling. The `attest-scorecard` job in `policy-check.yml@5ff2acd` declares `attestations: write` + `id-token: write`, which exceeds the wrapper's `contents:read + statuses:write` ceiling, even when `scorecard-emit: false` would skip the job at runtime. Closure path: (a) restructure `policy-check.yml` so `attest-scorecard` lives in a separate top-level workflow file (called workflows can't have job-scoped perms above caller ceiling regardless of `if:`), OR (b) once TF-023E-001 unblocks (repo public/org-owned), grant `attestations: write` + `id-token: write` universally at the wrapper level. (a) preserves least-privilege for opt-out adopters and is the recommended path. Surfaced at 023E fix-round-3 (the bumped wrapper failed startup_failure even after reverting `scorecard-emit: true` because the SHA bump alone trips the validation). Real adopters that opt in MUST follow the (b) path because they will universally grant the two permissions per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2.\\nSTATUS.md:261:\\nSTATUS.md:262:## Tracked-forward items from 020H.4 (rule-config canary)\\nSTATUS.md:263:\\nSTATUS.md:264:- **TF-020H4-001**: Extend `scripts/replay-canary.sh` registry tuple to include `expected_disabled_rules_count` field. Current tuple (verdict, findings, waivers) catches the primary regression class (suppress stops working → deny fires → conclusion=FAILURE + findings>=1) but misses a \\\"suppress-still-works-but-observability-record-dropped\\\" regression class. Closure path: extend the registry tuple to a 6th field, add a parse + verify branch in the script, update existing entries with the baseline disabled_rules counts. No deadline (forward-compat). Filed at 020H.4 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + completeness COMP-MIN-001 closure.\\nSTATUS.md:265:- **TF-020H4-002**: Repository Ruleset matching `canary/*` blocking merge to `main` from any `canary/` branch. Currently the suppression-canary PRs (Canary 3 PR + Canary 4 PR #81) are CI-green (the gate suppresses) and the only merge barrier is the DO-NOT-MERGE label + operator discipline. Bus-factor-1 risk: a single accidental click would merge a canary into main. Closure path: when a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), add a `scp-canary-no-merge-to-main` ruleset. Forward-compat — single-operator mode at v1.0.0 mitigates this risk via the same operator running both halves. Filed at 020H.4 R1 safety SAFE-MAJ-001 closure (architectural framing) + SAFE-nit-002 (canary branch protection).\\nSTATUS.md:266:- **TF-020H4-003**: `scripts/replay-canary.sh` error-handling hardening — (a) the `-1` sentinel for failed `gh run download` produces ambiguous output (REGRESSION rows could be either real drift or download failure); explicit ERROR rows would disambiguate; (b) `--measure-cold-start` mode dispatches a workflow run + sleeps 5 + reads \\\"latest\\\" with no race-protection — under concurrent invocations the wrong run could be read. Both pre-exist 020H.4 (landed in 020E.c) but surfaced by this slice's R1 safety review. Closure path: a hardening pass on replay-canary.sh in a future canary-touch slice. No deadline. Filed at 020H.4 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-001 + SAFE-MIN-002.\\nSTATUS.md:267:\\nSTATUS.md:268:## Recent decisions\\nSTATUS.md:269:\\nSTATUS.md:270:- **D-029 (2026-04-29)** — `policy-check.yml` permissions block adds `statuses: write` for the read-back commit-status.\\nSTATUS.md:271:- **D-030 (2026-04-30)** — apply 020J protections (tag-protection v* + required-signed-commits on main); idempotent applier at `scripts/configure-020j-protections.sh`.\\nSTATUS.md:272:- **D-031 (2026-04-30)** — adopt 020K personal-account / single-operator CODEOWNERS path.\\nSTATUS.md:273:- **D-032 (2026-04-30)** — apply 020D2 required-status-check + enforce_admins=true branch protection.\\nSTATUS.md:274:- **D-033 (2026-04-30)** — 020D2.1 reconciliation: required-check context renamed to `policy-check / scp/policy-check` (the rendered Actions check-run name); single-operator review shape corrected to `count=0` + `codeowner=false` (GitHub forbids PR self-review, locking out the operator under count=1).\\nSTATUS.md:275:- **D-034 (2026-04-30)** — 020F renovate-tag-protection: parallel ruleset on `refs/tags/renovate/v*` (deletion / non_fast_forward / update blocked, `enforcement: active`, `bypass_actors: []`). Closes 020F R1 safety review CRIT-SAFE-001.\\nSTATUS.md:276:- **D-035 (2026-04-30)** — 020G adopter-helper invocation procedure as estate doctrine: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` two-call PUT + POST shape, refuses CI, preserves `required_pull_request_reviews`, mandatory invocation-log commit at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`.\\nSTATUS.md:277:- **D-036 (2026-05-01)** — `policies/VERSIONING.md` MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH semver contract with one-release deprecation ramp AND `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/` RFC-lite process (quorum=1, 48h wall-clock window, auto-defer on zero approvals, **non-waivable window for bypass-introducing proposals** per 020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure) adopted as estate doctrine. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 (i)+(ii)+(iii) and BS-5.\\nSTATUS.md:278:- **D-037 (2026-05-01)** — Grant `issues: write` (job-level scope) to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` per slice 020H.3.1. The release-gate evaluation job remains `contents: read` only; the new job fires solely on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'`. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for readback). Closes WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001.\\nSTATUS.md:279:- **D-038 (2026-05-02)** — Adopt hash-pinned Python supply-chain installs across the SCP federation primitive's two YAML/JSON-validating workflows per slice 020M (`requirements/policy-check.{in,txt}`; `pip install --require-hashes`). PATCH bump v1.0.0 → v1.0.1. Closes TF-020H3rg-002.\\nSTATUS.md:280:- **D-039 (2026-05-02)** — Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow per slice 020N (`requirements/conflict-gate.{in,txt}`; `pip install --require-hashes` + `--no-deps` ct-auth + `--no-deps -e .`). No semver bump (conflict-gate.yml is internal-only per VERSIONING.md §\\\"Scope\\\"). Closes TF-020M-001.\\nSTATUS.md:281:- **D-040 (2026-05-02)** — Amend rule-RFC process so 48h is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) not a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Operator may merge as soon as adversarial-review fixpoint reached AND PR is open with `scp-rule-proposal` label. The non-waivable 48h MINIMUM for bypass-introducing proposals (020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001) is preserved. When 2nd maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation), 48h becomes mandatory MINIMUM. Surfaced by 020L dogfood when operator review of merge-readiness identified the wall-clock pause as theatre rather than safety in single-operator mode (no second pair of eyes for the pause to enable). Symmetric with D-033's \\\"operational reality must amend the spec\\\" pattern.\\nSTATUS.md:282:- **D-041 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B. MAJOR-pinned schema; default-false `scorecard-emit: bool` workflow_call input; OIDC artifact attestation via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0`; aggregator verifies at slice 023C via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow`. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema's `additionalProperties: false`). v1.1.0 → v1.2.0 (MINOR per VERSIONING.md additive-opt-in). Closes plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation.\\nSTATUS.md:283:- **D-042 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C. Per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (mandatory `job_workflow_ref` constraint pre-committed at 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits + CODEOWNERS + branch protection (NOT a separate Ed25519 layer; Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub). Two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) mirrors 023B's `attest-scorecard` job pattern. Internal-only; no semver bump. Closes plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation.\\nSTATUS.md:284:- **D-043 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D. Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` (deterministic golden-file-tested). MCP method has read-only contract per plan-doc invariant 5 (request: `repo_filter?` + `since_emitted_at?`; response: aggregated counts only, never waiver content per invariant 2; Pydantic-enforced field whitelist). ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. Closes plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation.\\nSTATUS.md:285:\\nSTATUS.md:286:## Post-Threshold-A backlog\\nSTATUS.md:287:\\nSTATUS.md:288:> **Naming note (per 020H.3 + 020H.3.1 disambiguation):** \\\"020H **part** 3\\\" (no dot, with the word \\\"part\\\") is the pre-Threshold-A v1.0.0-cut sequence slice that landed at PR #75 / commit `347fde2` (ADOPT-001 §12 federation appendix). \\\"020H**.**3\\\" (with dot) is the post-Threshold-A dot-N follow-up that landed at PR #79 / commit `42f49db` (release-gate workflow). \\\"020H**.**3.1\\\" (with two dots) is the reconciliation follow-up at this PR (release-gate auto-issue). The dot-N convention extends infinitely (020H.4, 020H.5, ...); the `part N` series is closed at part 3 (Threshold A).\\nSTATUS.md:289:\\nSTATUS.md:290:- ~~**020E.b**: post-protection canary evidence~~ ✅ landed 2026-04-30 (PR #66, commit `8248732`).\\nSTATUS.md:291:- ~~**020E.c**: waiver-suppression canary + `scripts/replay-canary.sh`~~ ✅ landed 2026-04-30 (PR #70, commit `4962bc9`); needed 2-PR fixpoint dance for warn-msg conftest bug.\\nSTATUS.md:292:- ~~**020F**: Renovate shared preset~~ ✅ landed 2026-04-30 (PR #71, commit `2743d4a`); 4-round R1 fixpoint surfaced CRIT-SAFE-001 (renovate/v* unprotected) → D-034 + new ruleset live; preset published at `renovate/v1.0.0`.\\nSTATUS.md:293:- ~~**020G**: branch-protection automation script for adopter onboarding~~ ✅ landed 2026-04-30 (PR #74, commit `373bcd2`); fix-round-1 closed 11 R1 findings + R2 + R3 + R4 fixpoint.\\nSTATUS.md:294:- ~~**020H part 3**: ADOPT-001 §12 federation-integration appendix (closes TF-D1-001..003)~~ ✅ landed 2026-04-30 (PR #75, commit `347fde2`); 6-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R3 (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ on all 3 lenses), 11 MAJ + 31 MIN + 11 nit closed. Opened TF-020H3-001..005.\\nSTATUS.md:295:- ~~**020H.1**: VERSIONING.md + rule-RFC process + rollback detection + version-manifest.json + freshness-warning emit~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 (PR #78, commit `06c2b61`); 2-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R2 (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ on all 3 lenses), 6 MAJ + 17 MIN + 11 nit closed. Filed D-036 + opened TF-020H1-001..005. Closed SCP-073.sec (SECURITY.md published).\\nSTATUS.md:296:- ~~**020H.2**: Regal binary SHA256 verification — closes TF-020H3-001~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 (PR #77, commit `bac1427`); 3-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R2 (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ), 3 MAJ + 5 MIN + 13 nit closed; closed 13 days ahead of the 2026-05-14 deadline.\\nSTATUS.md:297:- ~~**020H.3**: release-gate workflow (closes TF-005 + TF-020H1-001)~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 (PR #79, commit `42f49db`); 2-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R2 (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ on all 3 lenses), 1 CRIT + 7 MAJ + 10 MIN + 10 nit closed across 2 fix-rounds. Architectural framing: workflow is observer/annotator (post-tag), not pre-emptive gate; pre-emptive enforcement is the operator's mandatory dry-run pre-flight discipline. Opened TF-020H3rg-001..003.\\nSTATUS.md:298:- ~~**020H.3.1**: release-gate auto-issue (closes TF-020H3rg-003)~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 (PR #80, commit `d3e3c73`); 2-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R2 with R2 safety clean APPROVED (0 findings), 1 MAJ + 8 MIN + 6 nit + 1 fix-round-1 self-correction closed. Two-job split: release-gate (contents:read) + release-gate-violation-issue (issues:write at job level per D-037). Filed TF-020H3rg-004.\\nSTATUS.md:299:- ~~**020H.4**: rule-config disable canary (closes TF-020H1-004)~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 (PR #82, commit `c67a91a`); 2-round recursive review reaching fixpoint at R2 (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ on all 3 lenses), 1 MAJ + 6 MIN + 8 nit closed. New `canary/rule-config-disabled` branch (PR #81 DO-NOT-MERGE; SHA `841d350`) verified PASS at workflow run `25211284467`; `scripts/replay-canary.sh` registry extended; `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/canary-evidence.md` Canary 4 baseline section added. Suppression-path coverage triad complete (deny / waiver / rule-config). Opened TF-020H4-001..003.\\nSTATUS.md:300:- ~~**SCP-073.sec**: `SECURITY.md` publication (security disclosure path for policy-bypass reports)~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-01 in 020H.1 fix-round-1 (commit `3817384`); `SECURITY.md` at repo root with GitHub Security Advisory + `jimbrooke@me.com` channels, 3-business-day SLA, 30-day coordinated-disclosure target; ADOPT-001 §12.7.8 + `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/rule-regression.md` updated to reference it directly. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4.1 SCP-073.sec forward-looking flag.\\nSTATUS.md:301:- ~~**TF-020H3rg-002**: pip install --require-hashes for both YAML/JSON-validating workflows~~ ✅ landed 2026-05-02 in slice 020M (PR #84, commit `0e24cc6`); cut v1.0.1 PATCH.\\nSTATUS.md:302:- ~~**WP-SCP-022 proposal-queue**: structured proposal queue for new rules.~~ ✅ Phase 1 closed 2026-05-02 in slice **020L** (PR #89, commit `25db685`) — RFC-001 / SCP-R-004 (waiver `reason` must cite issue/PR URL) authored, R1+R2 fixpoint, merged via operator-discretion early-merge per D-040. ✅ **Phase 2** closed 2026-05-02 in slice **020P** (PR #91, commit `d83ce52`) — `policies/SCP-R-004.rego` + 21 Conftest tests + conflict-gate fixtures + workflow warn-baseline rendering + ADOPT-001 §12.7.14 + RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 + README.md §3 amendments + **v1.1.0 tag cut** (`https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/releases/tag/v1.1.0`). Closes TF-020L-002 + TF-020L-003. Filed TF-020P-001/002/003/004/005.\\nSTATUS.md:303:- **WP-SCP-023**: cross-repo scorecards.\\nSTATUS.md:304:- **WP-SCP-024**: estate cascade (FLA pilot → PIM/recommender/etc.).\\nSTATUS.md:305:\\nSTATUS.md:306:## Tracked-forward items from WP-SCP-024 024B-core / 024B-extras\\nSTATUS.md:307:\\nSTATUS.md:308:- **FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001** (open): per-repo dispatcher install pattern resolved in ACC. install_acc_hook.sh currently hardcodes ROOT_DIR via BASH_SOURCE so each adopter repo would need its own install ceremony. 024C MUST NOT open until closed. ACC-side work item; SCP-side awaits closure. Owner: @jrnb2024.\\nSTATUS.md:309:- **FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001** (open): 024B-extras workflow wiring will hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows, closing the residual operator-supplied `not applicable` bypass surface. 024B-core keeps the CLI guard + documentation only; the workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path.\\nSTATUS.md:310:- **TF-024B-SCAFFOLDER-023E002-UPDATE** (open): when TF-023E-002 closes, remove the post-v1.2.0 warning block from `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` and delete the cutover ancestry guard that keeps the v1.0.0 fallback recommendation alive. The scaffolder should stop warning only after the separate `attest-scorecard` workflow restructuring lands. Owner: @jrnb2024.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1:# ADOPT-001 — Standards Control Plane Project Onboarding\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:2:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:3:**Version:** 0.1\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:4:**Status:** Active\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:5:**Date:** 2026-04-12\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:6:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:7:## 1. Purpose\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:8:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:9:This is the single onboarding brief for any repo that wants to adopt the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:10:Standards Control Plane.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:11:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:12:If a team asks, \\\"What do we need to change to use SCP in our repo, wire it into\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:13:CI, and update our governance docs?\\\", point them here.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:14:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:15:The goal is not to redesign the adopter repo. The goal is to plug SCP into the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:16:repo's existing delivery process in a way that is:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:17:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:18:- structured\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:19:- reviewable\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:20:- advisory first\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:21:- easy to run locally and in CI\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:22:- compatible with stronger existing repo governance\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:23:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:24:Repos that host an HTTP service audited by SCP also take on the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:25:service-lifecycle adoption obligation: `services.yml` must declare an\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:26:`auth_contract` block conforming to SVC-003 (see §11).\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:27:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:28:## 2. What Adopting Teams Get\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:29:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:30:After onboarding, a project should be able to:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:31:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:32:1. ask for targeted standards guidance before coding\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:33:2. run changed-file audit in PR workflows\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:34:3. run full or subsystem audit on a schedule\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:35:4. keep findings, waivers, and reports in a consistent shape\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:36:5. layer repo-specific standards on top of the shared registry\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:37:6. publish CI-facing and Control Tower-facing outputs without a second audit path\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:38:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:39:## 3. Estate Best-Practice Position\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:40:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:41:Adopt SCP using the same broad discipline already proven across Control Tower,\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:42:Documentation Intelligence, and the estate conformance work:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:43:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:44:- use a shared standard, not copy-pasted local folklore\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:45:- extract and reuse proven patterns rather than inventing a second mechanism\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:46:- keep the integration layer thin; do not refactor core business logic just to\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:47:  \\\"make SCP fit\\\"\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:48:- keep audit local to the repo that owns the code\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:49:- keep findings structured and durable; markdown is a projection, not the source\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:50:  of truth\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:51:- keep the rollout advisory first; do not introduce hard CI blocking at\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:52:  adoption time\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:53:- keep project overlays explicit instead of weakening the shared standard into\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:54:  vague prose\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:55:- keep the existing repo governance protocol if it is stronger; SCP supplements\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:56:  governance, it does not replace it\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:57:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:58:## 4. Governance Rules Teams Must Preserve\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:59:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:60:The minimum expected governance posture for adopters is:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:61:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:62:1. do not silently descope\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:63:2. do not skip the repo's existing review process\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:64:3. every SCP finding is resolved, waived, accepted, marked false positive, or\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:65:   explicitly deferred by the repo's normal governance path\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:66:4. docs and control records are updated in the same session as code changes\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:67:5. git stays clean: branch, PR, merge, and artifact ownership remain explicit\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:68:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:69:If a repo already follows the stronger Control Tower delivery protocol with\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:70:parallel adversarial review, keep using it.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:71:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:72:## 5. Recommended Deployment Model\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:73:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:74:Use this deployment model unless there is a strong repo-specific reason not to:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:75:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:76:### 5.1 Package and CLI first\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:77:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:78:Install SCP as a Python tool dependency in the adopter repo and call it through\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:79:CLI commands in local workflows and CI.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:80:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:81:This is the default and recommended mode.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:82:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:83:### 5.2 Repo-local audit\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:84:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:85:Run `audit-changed` and `audit` inside the target repo, not in a central shared\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:86:service. The repo that owns the code should own extraction, normalisation, and\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:87:artifact generation.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:88:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:89:### 5.3 Optional shared consult service\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:90:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:91:If teams want a shared consult endpoint, run `serve` against a Control Tower\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:92:OIDC audience and use it for consult and registry access. Do not move the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:93:canonical audit runtime there yet. Raw `--auth-token` bearer auth is\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:94:deprecated — machine callers should use Control Tower agent keys\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:95:(`mode.api_key`). See §11.6 for the mode-selection guide and §11.7 for\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:96:the bearer deprecation window.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:97:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:98:### 5.4 Control Tower consumes artifacts\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:99:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:100:Control Tower should read the emitted artifacts. It should not invoke evaluator\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:101:runtime directly.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:102:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:103:### 5.5 Docs-agent remains optional\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:104:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:105:Docs-agent is a future enrichment layer for broader retrieval and historical\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:106:evidence. It is not required for base onboarding.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:107:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:108:## 6. Pre-Onboarding Decisions\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:109:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:110:Before implementation begins, each adopting repo must decide the following:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:111:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:112:| Decision | Why it matters |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:113:|----------|----------------|\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:114:| **Repo owner** | Owns the adoption and follow-up fixes |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:115:| **Governance owner** | Owns waivers, accepted debt, and process wording |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:116:| **Overlay owner** | Owns repo-specific standards and overlay drift |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:117:| **Pilot subsystem** | Gives consult and audit a bounded first target |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:118:| **Primary domains** | Start with `governance,architecture` unless there is a specific reason not to |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:119:| **CI owner** | Wires PR and scheduled audit jobs |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:120:| **Agent instruction file** | Defines where consult-before-coding will be added (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, or equivalent) |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:121:| **Service mode decision** | Decide whether local CLI is enough or whether the repo wants the optional HTTP service |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:122:| **Service auth modes (SVC-003)** | If the repo hosts an HTTP service audited by SCP, decide which of the four approved `auth_contract` modes it accepts (see §11). `mode.user_oidc` for browsers, `mode.api_key` for machine callers, `mode.service_rs256` for S2S, `mode.bearer_legacy` only with a time-bound migration waiver |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:123:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:124:## 7. Required Repo Changes\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:125:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:126:Every adopter should make the following changes.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:127:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:128:### 7.1 Install SCP as a pinned tool dependency\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:129:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:130:Until SCP is published to an internal package index, the simplest path is to pin\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:131:the GitHub repo:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:132:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:133:```bash\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:134:pip install \\\"git+https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-.git@main\\\"\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:135:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:136:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:137:Best practice:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:138:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:139:- pin the Git reference or released version explicitly\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:140:- do not install from a floating local copy in CI\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:141:- keep SCP version changes visible in dependency review\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:142:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:143:Installing SCP pulls in a small transitive closure at runtime. The\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:144:load-bearing runtime dependencies are:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:145:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:146:- `fastapi`, `uvicorn`, `httpx` — the HTTP surface\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:147:- `jsonschema>=4.25.0` — schema validation\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:148:- `pyyaml>=6.0` — `services.yml` parsing (required once SVC-001/002/003\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:149:  are in use; see §11)\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:150:- `pydantic>=2.12.0`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:151:- the vendored `ct_auth-0.8.0` wheel — Control Tower OIDC + BFF helpers\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:152:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:153:No action is required beyond `pip install` — but teams with locked\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:154:dependency sets should know these are on the list before SCP lands.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:155:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:156:### 7.2 Add a repo-local overlay\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:157:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:158:Create a repo-local overlay directory so project-specific rules and patterns can\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:159:sharpen the shared registry without editing SCP core.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:160:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:161:Recommended location:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:162:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:163:```text\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:164:governance/standards-control-plane/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:165:  standards-index.json\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:166:  architecture/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:167:    index.json\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:168:    rules/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:169:    patterns/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:170:  governance/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:171:    index.json\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:172:    rules/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:173:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:174:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:175:Use overlays for:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:176:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:177:- repo-specific boundary rules\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:178:- stricter severity for already-shared rules\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:179:- approved local patterns\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:180:- project terminology or subsystem tags\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:181:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:182:Do not use overlays for:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:183:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:184:- broad restatement of the shared registry\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:185:- undocumented local preferences with no owner\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:186:- hiding or weakening shared standards that should remain estate-wide\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:187:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:188:### 7.3 Add thin wrapper scripts\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:189:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:190:Do not scatter raw long CLI invocations across prompts, READMEs, and CI files.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:191:Create repo-local wrapper scripts with the repo's fixed defaults.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:192:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:193:Recommended wrappers:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:194:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:195:```text\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:196:scripts/scp-consult\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:197:scripts/scp-audit-pr\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:198:scripts/scp-audit-full\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:199:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:200:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:201:Those wrappers should own:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:202:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:203:- subsystem default\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:204:- overlay path\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:205:- standards version\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:206:- any repo-specific request-file path\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:207:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:208:### 7.4 Add findings and waiver ownership\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:209:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:210:The repo must own these artifacts:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:211:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:212:- `output/findings/waivers.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:213:- `output/findings/open-findings.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:214:- `output/findings/findings-history.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:215:- `output/findings/area-summaries/*.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:216:- `output/reports/latest-review.md`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:217:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:218:Best practice:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:219:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:220:- commit waivers and long-lived findings/history artifacts through normal PR flow\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:221:- treat PR-scoped CI summaries as build artifacts, not as mandatory committed files\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:222:- keep waiver approvals explicit and time-bound\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:223:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:224:### 7.5 Update governance docs\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:225:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:226:Every adopter should update:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:227:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:228:- `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, or equivalent agent instruction file\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:229:- repo governance protocol or delivery guide\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:230:- PR template or delivery checklist if one exists\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:231:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:232:## 8. Governance Doc Wording to Add\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:233:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:234:At minimum, the adopting repo should add a section equivalent to this:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:235:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:236:```text\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:237:## Standards Control Plane Usage\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:238:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:239:Before implementation:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:240:1. Identify impacted subsystem, area, and files.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:241:2. Run SCP consult for the relevant domains.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:242:3. Read applicable rules, approved patterns, and open findings.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:243:4. Summarise the implementation plan against those constraints before coding.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:244:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:245:Before PR or handoff:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:246:1. Run SCP changed-file audit.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:247:2. Review findings and fix them where required.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:248:3. Record waivers or accepted debt explicitly; do not silently bypass findings.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:249:4. Keep generated findings and report artifacts aligned with the code change.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:250:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:251:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:252:If the repo already has stronger governance language, integrate SCP into that\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:253:language rather than replacing it.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:254:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:255:## 9. Agent Workflow Teams Should Adopt\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:256:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:257:This is the expected operating loop for implementation agents:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:258:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:259:1. identify impacted subsystem, area, and likely changed files\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:260:2. run `consult` (include `service-lifecycle` in `domains` when the repo\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:261:   hosts an HTTP service or when the change touches `services.yml`)\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:262:3. read:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:263:   - applicable rules\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:264:   - approved patterns\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:265:   - open findings\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:266:   - relevant historical review references\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:267:4. summarise the plan against those constraints\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:268:5. implement\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:269:6. run `audit-changed`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:270:7. fix findings or explicitly record exceptions through the repo's governance path\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:271:8. only then open or update the PR\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:272:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:273:SCP should become part of the normal pre-coding and pre-PR loop. It should not\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:274:be a late afterthought.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:275:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:276:## 10. CI Integration\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:277:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:278:### 10.1 PR workflow\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:279:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:280:Each adopting repo should add a non-blocking PR job that runs changed-file audit.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:281:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:282:Recommended command shape:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:283:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:284:```bash\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:285:standards-control-plane audit-changed \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:286:  --base-ref origin/main \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:287:  --head-ref HEAD \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:288:  --domains governance,architecture \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:289:  --subsystem <subsystem> \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:290:  --standards-version <registry-version> \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:291:  --overlay governance/standards-control-plane \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:292:  --write-output\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:293:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:294:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:295:Then publish:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:296:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:297:- `output/ci/latest-ci.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:298:- `output/ci/latest-ci.md`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:299:- `output/reports/latest-review.md`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:300:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:301:Do not fail the build on day one. Warnings should be visible first.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:302:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:303:### 10.2 Scheduled workflow\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:304:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:305:Each adopting repo should also add a scheduled job for broader refresh:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:306:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:307:```bash\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:308:standards-control-plane audit \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:309:  --request governance/scp/audit-request.json \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:310:  --overlay governance/standards-control-plane \\\\\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:311:  --write-output\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:312:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:313:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:314:Recommended cadence:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:315:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:316:- nightly for fast-moving repos\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:317:- weekly for slower repos\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:318:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:319:### 10.3 Control Tower projection\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:320:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:321:If the repo participates in estate-level reporting, publish or expose:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:322:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:323:- `output/control-tower/surface.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:324:- `output/control-tower/estate-dashboard.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:325:- `output/control-tower/subsystems/*.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:326:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:327:## 11. Service Auth Contract (SVC-003)\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:328:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:329:SVC-003 defines a closed four-mode auth contract every estate service\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:330:must declare in its `services.yml`. This section has two tracks:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:331:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:332:- **Consumer track (§11.2)** — adopters who only *call* SCP's HTTP\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:333:  service (or any other estate service). Read §11.1 and §11.2 and\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:334:  skip the producer subsections.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:335:- **Producer track (§11.3–§11.8)** — adopters who *host* an HTTP\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:336:  service audited by SCP. Read everything: the services.yml\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:337:  declaration shape, per-mode guidance, the `--auth-token`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:338:  deprecation window, and the upgrade path from pre-SVC-003\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:339:  manifests.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:340:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:341:SCP's own `services.yml` at repo root and `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-019/\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:342:dogfood-scp.md` are the canonical producer example.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:343:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:344:> **Operative close date update — 2026-04-20.** Per SCP's Option-B\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:345:> signal (`docs/reviews/WP-SCP-019/d019-option-b-signal.md`, filed\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:346:> 2026-04-20), the estate-wide `mode.bearer_legacy` deprecation close\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:347:> date has **operationally slid from 2026-06-30 to 2026-09-30** for\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:348:> consumer-team planning and outbound comms from 2026-04-20 forward.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:349:> The formal amending decision (D-021) records on 2026-05-31 with\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:350:> observed adoption-PR evidence substituted. D-019's 2026-06-30\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:351:> citation in this section, the `deprecation_close_date` example\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:352:> snippet in §11.7, and the SCP-specific close-date statement in\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:353:> §11.7 all remain as-ratified until D-021 fires — substitute\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:354:> `2026-09-30` when authoring new `services.yml` / `waivers.json`\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:355:> entries from this signal forward. Threshold for the 2026-05-31\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:356:> check (whether Option B holds or D-019 stands) was CT-confirmed\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:357:> verbatim on 2026-04-20; corner cases §2.1–§2.3 in\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:358:> `SCP-CONFIRM-D-019-THRESHOLD-2026-04-20.md` apply.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:359:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:360:### 11.1 Approved modes (closed set)\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:361:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:362:Custom modes are not permitted; a new mode requires a standards\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:363:change. The static evaluator scans for the implementation-marker\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:364:patterns listed below; Node/Go services must produce source patterns\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:365:matching the same substrings to satisfy the code-pattern scan.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:366:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:367:| Mode ID | Use | Shape | Implementation markers |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:368:|---------|-----|-------|------------------------|\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:369:| `mode.user_oidc` | Browser flows | Control Tower OIDC + JWKS verification + `aud=<app-id>` claim + httpOnly cookie session | `ControlTowerAuth`, `ct_auth.`, `create_bff_routes`, `CT_JWKS_URL` |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:370:| `mode.service_rs256` | Service-to-service (target state; replaces HS256) | S2S RS256 + required `aud` claim + JWKS verification | `algorithms=[\\\"RS256\\\"]`, `algorithms=['RS256']`, `algorithm=\\\"RS256\\\"`, `algorithm='RS256'` |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:371:| `mode.api_key` | Agents, CLIs, machine callers | Control Tower-issued agent key, revocable via CT, `X-API-Key` header | `X-API-Key`, `x-api-key` |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:372:| `mode.bearer_legacy` | Deprecated. Pre-SVC-003 raw bearer token | Requires per-service migration waiver with explicit `deprecation_close_date` | `secrets.compare_digest` |\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:373:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:374:The reference implementation for `mode.user_oidc` is SCP's own\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:375:service at `src/standards_control_plane/service.py` (commit\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:376:`66ba8a4`): vendored `ct_auth` wheel, `ControlTowerAuth` with\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:377:`jwks_url` + `audience`, `create_bff_routes` for the cookie session\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:378:flow.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:379:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:380:### 11.2 Consumer track: calling SCP (or any SVC-003 service)\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:381:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:382:Find the target service's `services.yml` (SCP's lives at the root of\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:383:`standards-control-plane`). The `auth_contract.accepted_modes` list\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:384:tells you which headers your caller must send.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:385:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:386:- **Browser**: perform the Control Tower OIDC login flow at the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:387:  service's `/auth/login`; subsequent requests carry the httpOnly\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:388:  cookie session the BFF sets. No explicit header from your code.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:389:- **Machine caller (`mode.api_key`)**: request an agent key from\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:390:  Control Tower's agent-key admin surface and send\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:391:  `X-API-Key: <key>` on every request. Keys are revocable via CT.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:392:- **Machine caller (`mode.bearer_legacy`, deprecated)**: during the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:393:  deprecation window (close date per D-019 is 2026-06-30), send\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:394:  `Authorization: Bearer <token>` using the token the target service\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:395:  configured via `--auth-token`. Plan your migration to\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:396:  `mode.api_key` before the close date — after it, SVC-003 fires\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:397:  `bearer-legacy-close-date-passed` on the target service.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:398:- **S2S caller (`mode.service_rs256`)**: mint an RS256 JWT with\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:399:  `aud` set to the target service's `services.yml` key and send it\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:400:  in the Authorization header.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:401:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:402:The producer subsections below describe the shape a service\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:403:publishes; as a consumer, you only need the declaration's mode list\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:404:and the issuance endpoint for each mode.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:405:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:406:### 11.3 Producer track: upgrading a pre-SVC-003 services.yml\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:407:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:408:If your repo already has a `services.yml` from WP-SCP-018 or earlier\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:409:and it has no `auth_contract` block, SVC-003's auto-check will fire\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:410:`missing-auth-contract` against every non-planned service. The\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:411:minimum upgrade:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:412:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:413:1. Pick the auth mode(s) your service actually accepts today. If it\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:414:   still uses `--auth-token`, that is `mode.bearer_legacy` and needs\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:415:   the waiver shape in §11.7.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:416:2. Add the `auth_contract` block under each service's\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:417:   `runtime_contract` (see §11.4–§11.7 for per-mode shape).\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:418:3. Run `standards-control-plane audit --request <your-request>.json\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:419:   --domains service-lifecycle` and address any findings.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:420:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:421:Upgrading one service at a time is supported — other services fire\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:422:their own `missing-auth-contract` until they are declared, but the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:423:rule scope is per-service.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:424:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:425:### 11.4 Producer track: mode.user_oidc\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:426:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:427:Register an OAuth client with Control Tower for each environment\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:428:(e.g. `my-app`, `my-app-dev`). Control Tower registration covers the\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:429:redirect URIs and CORS origins; SCP's `docs/deployment.md` §3 is a\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:430:worked example for reference.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:431:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:432:Set runtime env vars on the service process:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:433:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:434:```text\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:435:AUTH_ENABLED=true\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:436:CT_BASE_URL=https://control-tower.brokapps.ai\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:437:CT_APP_ID=my-app-dev\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:438:PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://my-app-dev.brokapps.ai\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:439:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:440:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:441:`CT_JWKS_URL` is optional — if unset, it defaults to\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:442:`<CT_BASE_URL>/api/v1/.well-known/jwks.json`. Override only if your\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:443:Control Tower deployment publishes JWKS at a non-standard path.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:444:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:445:Declare the mode in `services.yml`. The `audience` value must match\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:446:the service's `services.yml` key in the target environment. SCP\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:447:solves the \\\"local key vs staging key\\\" mismatch by using distinct\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:448:service keys per environment (e.g. `scp-dev` locally, `scp` in\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:449:staging); adopters should do the same rather than using a shared\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:450:key with divergent audiences.\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:451:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:452:```yaml\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:453:services:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:454:  my-app-dev:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:455:    healthcheck: /health\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:456:    local:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:457:      status: ready\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:458:      runtime_contract:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:459:        # ... SVC-001 fields ...\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:460:        auth_contract:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:461:          accepted_modes:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:462:            - mode: mode.user_oidc\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:463:              audience: my-app-dev\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:464:              jwks_url: https://control-tower.brokapps.ai/api/v1/.well-known/jwks.json\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:465:```\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:466:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:467:In Python services, reuse the vendored `ct_auth` wheel:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:468:\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:469:```python\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:470:from ct_auth.bff import create_bff_routes\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:471:from cG INVARIANT comment accurately notes self-protection rule must remain last\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"PASS\\\",\\\"detail\\\":\\\"CODEOWNERS lines 23-31 contain the ORDERING INVARIANT block. Lines 28-30 explicitly state: 'The /CODEOWNERS self-protection rule is the final line by design \\u2014 it must remain last so any reordering attempt routes through @jrnb2024 review. Re-verify ordering whenever this file is touched.'\\\"},{\\\"item\\\":6,\\\"description\\\":\\\"tests/** breadth \\u2014 does it cover things that should not be governance-reviewed\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 NO NARROWING RECOMMENDED\\\",\\\"detail\\\":\\\"All top-level test_*.py files touch governance-critical logic: test_waivers_and_scoring.py (waiver bypass paths), test_governance_audit.py (audit enforcement), test_scp_dogfood.py (SCP self-compliance), test_findings_persistence.py (findings tamper detection), test_review_evidence.py (evidence integrity). No test file is purely cosmetic. In single-operator mode reviewer overhead is self-imposed and negligible. The broad tests/** rule is appropriate and should not be narrowed.\\\"},{\\\"item\\\":7,\\\"description\\\":\\\".github/** breadth \\u2014 are issue templates and PR templates governance-sensitive enough\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"PASS\\\",\\\"detail\\\":\\\"No ISSUE_TEMPLATE directory or PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md currently exists. When introduced, issue templates define the channel for bypass/waiver requests (governance-sensitive); PR templates define merge-process expectations (governance-sensitive). Requiring @jrnb2024 review on these is proportionate. .github/** breadth is appropriate.\\\"},{\\\"item\\\":8,\\\"description\\\":\\\"/CODEOWNERS leading-slash semantics \\u2014 root-only vs anywhere in tree\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"PASS with nit (R3-SAFE-018)\\\",\\\"detail\\\":\\\"Leading slash in '/CODEOWNERS' correctly matches only the root-level CODEOWNERS file, not CODEOWNERS files at any subdirectory depth. Intent is to protect the root governance file \\u2014 semantics match intent. .github/CODEOWNERS (if introduced) is covered by .github/**. docs/CODEOWNERS (if introduced) is NOT covered by any rule \\u2014 see R3-SAFE-018.\\\"},{\\\"item\\\":9,\\\"description\\\":\\\"\\u00a74 020K spec text self-consistency \\u2014 canonical vs broadened paths\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"PASS with nit (R3-SAFE-019)\\\",\\\"detail\\\":\\\"Spec text correctly identifies canonical-minimum paths and their broadened replacements with closing finding IDs. The actual CODEOWNERS contains only the broadened forms. However the 'PLUS' conjunction could be misread as requiring both sets as separate entries. See R3-SAFE-019 for mitigation.\\\"}]}\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020k/review-safety-r3.json:82:  \"stdout_tail\": \"t as separate CODEOWNERS entries. In fact the broadened rules replace (subsume) the canonical-minimum paths \\u2014 '.github/**' replaces '.github/workflows/**'; 'output/findings/**' replaces 'output/findings/waivers.json'; 'tests/**' replaces 'tests/conflict_gate/**'. The current CODEOWNERS correctly contains only the broadened rules, but the spec text could mislead a future automated agent into reintroducing the superseded narrower rules as additional entries.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Plan \\u00a74 020K (line 114) reads: 'canonical-minimum set policies/**, renovate/**, .github/workflows/**, docs/DECISIONS.md, output/findings/waivers.json PLUS the safety-review-broadened set \\u2026 tests/** (broadened from tests/conflict_gate/**) \\u2026 .github/** (broadened from .github/workflows/**) \\u2026 output/findings/** (broadened from output/findings/waivers.json)'. CODEOWNERS does NOT contain '.github/workflows/**', 'output/findings/waivers.json', or 'tests/conflict_gate/**' as separate entries; only the broadened forms are present. The 'PLUS' conjunction does not communicate replacement.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"No current safety impact. If a future automated agent applies the spec literally and adds '.github/workflows/**', 'tests/conflict_gate/**', and 'output/findings/waivers.json' as additional rules, they would be redundant (subsumed by the broader rules; last-match-wins means no coverage change). However, the extra rules create noise, could confuse ordering-invariant reasoning, and signal to reviewers that the canonical-minimum paths should be present independently \\u2014 obscuring which rule is actually governing each path.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Revise the \\u00a74 020K spec sentence to clarify that broadened rules replace rather than supplement the canonical-minimum paths. Example reframing: 'canonical-minimum paths (policies/**, renovate/**, .github/workflows/**, docs/DECISIONS.md, output/findings/waivers.json) \\u2014 each superseded path is replaced by its broader form below; the CODEOWNERS contains only the broadened form as a single entry per governance area'.\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"observations\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"OBS-R3-001\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"note\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"docs/reviews/** is not covered by any CODEOWNERS rule. Glob confirms docs/reviews/ contains substantive audit-evidence files (acceptance_verification.md, review_findings.md, adversarial-review-round-N.md) across all WP-SCP-001 through WP-SCP-022. These can be modified without @jrnb2024 review. This gap pre-dates Fix Round 2 and was not surfaced by R1 or R2 safety reviews; it is not a new finding introduced by Fix Round 2 and does not affect the fixpoint verdict. In single-operator mode, self-tampering of audit trail is an accepted operational exposure. Recommended for consideration in a future CODEOWNERS hygiene pass: add 'docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024'.\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"specific_verification_results\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tests/** covers tests/workflow/**, tests/scp_mcp/**, tests/scripts/**, top-level test_*.py\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CODEOWNERS line 36 'tests/** @jrnb2024'. All four named sub-trees confirmed present via glob. Top-level test_*.py files (20 confirmed) covered.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":2,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\".github/** covers .github/dependabot.yml, .github/workflows/**, future .github/CODEOWNERS and issue-templates\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CODEOWNERS line 35 '.github/** @jrnb2024'. dependabot.yml confirmed present. Three workflow files confirmed. No ISSUE_TEMPLATE or PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE currently exists; wildcard covers them if introduced. .github/CODEOWNERS does not exist; covered if introduced.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":3,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/CODEOWNERS @jrnb2024 is the final line of the governance block; last-match-wins ensures self-protection\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Line 49 is the last line. Comment on lines 46-48 explicitly states 'Last line by design'. No trailing blank lines or rules follow it. Last-match-wins correctly means this rule governs the root CODEOWNERS file even if a future broad wildcard precedes it.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":4,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"docs/integrations/** is covered\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CODEOWNERS line 42 'docs/integrations/** @jrnb2024'. docs/integrations/mcp-error-codes.md and docs/integrations/conflict-gate.md both confirmed present and covered.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":5,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ORDERING INVARIANT comment accurately notes self-protection rule must remain last\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CODEOWNERS lines 23-31 contain the ORDERING INVARIANT block. Lines 28-30 explicitly state: 'The /CODEOWNERS self-protection rule is the final line by design \\u2014 it must remain last so any reordering attempt routes through @jrnb2024 review. Re-verify ordering whenever this file is touched.'\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":6,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tests/** breadth \\u2014 does it cover things that should not be governance-reviewed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS \\u2014 NO NARROWING RECOMMENDED\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"All top-level test_*.py files touch governance-critical logic: test_waivers_and_scoring.py (waiver bypass paths), test_governance_audit.py (audit enforcement), test_scp_dogfood.py (SCP self-compliance), test_findings_persistence.py (findings tamper detection), test_review_evidence.py (evidence integrity). No test file is purely cosmetic. In single-operator mode reviewer overhead is self-imposed and negligible. The broad tests/** rule is appropriate and should not be narrowed.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":7,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\".github/** breadth \\u2014 are issue templates and PR templates governance-sensitive enough\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"No ISSUE_TEMPLATE directory or PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md currently exists. When introduced, issue templates define the channel for bypass/waiver requests (governance-sensitive); PR templates define merge-process expectations (governance-sensitive). Requiring @jrnb2024 review on these is proportionate. .github/** breadth is appropriate.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":8,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/CODEOWNERS leading-slash semantics \\u2014 root-only vs anywhere in tree\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS with nit (R3-SAFE-018)\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Leading slash in '/CODEOWNERS' correctly matches only the root-level CODEOWNERS file, not CODEOWNERS files at any subdirectory depth. Intent is to protect the root governance file \\u2014 semantics match intent. .github/CODEOWNERS (if introduced) is covered by .github/**. docs/CODEOWNERS (if introduced) is NOT covered by any rule \\u2014 see R3-SAFE-018.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":9,\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\u00a74 020K spec text self-consistency \\u2014 canonical vs broadened paths\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PASS with nit (R3-SAFE-019)\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Spec text correctly identifies canonical-minimum paths and their broadened replacements with closing finding IDs. The actual CODEOWNERS contains only the broadened forms. However the 'PLUS' conjunction could be misread as requiring both sets as separate entries. See R3-SAFE-019 for mitigation.\\\\\\\"}]}\\\",\\\"stop_reason\\\":\\\"end_turn\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"ad006585-ce9b-4ae5-83c9-c67b82e5338a\\\",\\\"total_cost_usd\\\":0.5532445500000001,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":6,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":47667,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":85591,\\\"output_tokens\\\":23143,\\\"server_tool_use\\\":{\\\"web_search_requests\\\":0,\\\"web_fetch_requests\\\":0},\\\"service_tier\\\":\\\"standard\\\",\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":47667,\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0},\\\"inference_geo\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"iterations\\\":[{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":5334,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":36209,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":19329,\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":19329},\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\"}],\\\"speed\\\":\\\"standard\\\"},\\\"modelUsage\\\":{\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1488,\\\"outputTokens\\\":33,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.001653,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":6,\\\"outputTokens\\\":23143,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":85591,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":47667,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.55159155,\\\"contextWindow\\\":600000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"3b05e94f-3de9-4696-9477-bdaff4a3d496\\\"}\\n\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020k/review-safety-r3.json:106:        \"note\": \"docs/reviews/** is not covered by any CODEOWNERS rule. Glob confirms docs/reviews/ contains substantive audit-evidence files (acceptance_verification.md, review_findings.md, adversarial-review-round-N.md) across all WP-SCP-001 through WP-SCP-022. These can be modified without @jrnb2024 review. This gap pre-dates Fix Round 2 and was not surfaced by R1 or R2 safety reviews; it is not a new finding introduced by Fix Round 2 and does not affect the fixpoint verdict. In single-operator mode, self-tampering of audit trail is an accepted operational exposure. Recommended for consideration in a future CODEOWNERS hygiene pass: add 'docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024'.\"\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020k/review-safety-r3.json:196:    \"summary\": \"All five Round-2 safety findings (R2-SAFE-013 through R2-SAFE-017) are verified closed against the current CODEOWNERS and plan text. Fix-round 2 broadened two rules (tests/** and .github/**) and added two new rules (docs/integrations/**, /CODEOWNERS) \\u2014 every change widens coverage and introduces no bypass regression. Two new nit observations surface: (1) docs/CODEOWNERS is a GitHub-recognised secondary CODEOWNERS location not covered by any rule; it is dormant while the root file exists, but its uncovered introduction creates a latent confusion vector; (2) the \\u00a74 020K spec text 'canonical-minimum PLUS broadened' framing implies both rule sets should coexist, when in fact broadened rules replace the canonical-minimum paths. One pre-existing observation (not new from Fix Round 2) is noted for completeness: docs/reviews/** is not covered. No new MAJ or CRIT findings. 020K meets the stated fixpoint criterion.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"CORRECTNESS R1 review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020P (RULE-001 Phase 2 — implement SCP-R-004 + cut v1.1.0) at branch feature/wp-scp-022-020p-scp-r-004-implementation, HEAD commit `9f8639d`. The slice surface is large but mostly mechanical translation from RULE-001 (the canonical specification merged at PR #89, commit 25db685) into Rego + tests + workflow + version + docs. Verify CORRECTNESS exhaustively. Specifically: (1) policies/SCP-R-004.rego matches RULE-001 §3.1 match conditions + §3.4 implementation sketch + §3.3 annotation contract: own scp_r_004_is_waiver_payload + scp_r_004_has_nonempty_string + scp_r_004_has_url + scp_r_004_truncate_reason predicates (NOT cross-rule scp_r_002_* per SAFE-MAJ-001 closure); regex `https?://[^\\\\s]+`; sprintf message includes index + entry rule_id + entry finding_id + truncated reason. (2) The truncation predicate scp_r_004_truncate_reason: `count(reason) <= 80` returns reason as-is; `count(reason) > 80` returns `concat('', [substring(reason, 0, 79), '…'])`. Verify `substring(reason, 0, 79)` actually returns 79 chars (Rego substring semantics: substring(s, start, length)) — this gives 79 chars of body + 1 char ellipsis = 80 total. Verify the ellipsis is U+2026 (three-dot ellipsis) and not three ASCII dots. (3) Conftest tests at policies/tests/scp_r_004_test.rego: 19 cases. Verify each case actually exercises what its docstring claims. Specifically the meta-waiver test: the waivers data has `rule_id: SCP-R-004` and the waiver's OWN reason contains a URL — verify the test would FAIL if the meta-waiver's reason had no URL (which would mean the meta-waiver itself fires SCP-R-004 against the waivers data). Verify the truncation test (test_scp_r_004_truncates_long_reason_in_message) actually exercises the >80-char path AND that the assertion `not contains(results[0].message, \\\"by quite a bit and should be truncated\\\")` validates the truncation cut point. (4) Conflict-gate fixtures at tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/{allow,deny}/{input.json, expected-verdict.json}. Verify expected-verdict.json shape matches the fixtures for SCP-R-001/002 (same `rule_id`, `file`, `verdict`, `reason` keys). (5) Python evaluator at tests/conflict_gate/test_conflict_gate.py `_evaluate_scp_r_004_python`: verify URL_PATTERN regex `https?://\\\\S+` is semantically equivalent to Rego `https?://[^\\\\s]+` for the ASCII fixture corpus (they should be — Python re `\\\\S` and RE2 `[^\\\\s]` agree on ASCII). (6) Workflow integration at .github/workflows/policy-check.yml: WARN_BASELINE_RULES = {'SCP-R-004'} set; warn-baseline branch emits `::warning::` annotations; effective_denies excludes warn-baseline rules; threshold check `if effective_denies and SCP_THRESHOLD == 'deny'` does NOT include warn_baseline_findings. Verify the YAML is syntactically valid (the change is inside a python3 heredoc — verify the heredoc shell-escaping is correct). (7) version-manifest.json: version 1.0.1 → 1.1.0; minor 1.0 → 1.1; major 1; released_at 2026-05-02. Verify the JSON is valid. (8) docs/releases/v1.1.0.md: covers what ships, what changes for adopters, files in release, how adopters can verify. Verify RULE-001 references are correct. (9) docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md §12.7.14: 3 adopter response options + meta-waiver shape requirement. Verify the rule-config disable example YAML is syntactically valid + matches schemas/rule-config.schema.json. (10) RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 amendment: residual-bypass guidance for syntactic-pattern rules. Verify the worked-example pointer to RULE-001 §5 case 5 is accurate. (11) README.md §3 amendment: 'new domain' definition. Verify the same-input-schema framing matches RULE-001 §9 framing. (12) DISPATCH-NOTE.md scope decision is internally consistent. (13) Verify NO regressions on SCP-R-001/002/003 — read those rule files briefly to confirm the SCP-R-004 changes don't introduce any package-level naming collisions (e.g. duplicate `scp_r_004_*` symbols). (14) The truncation '…' character: verify the Rego file uses `…` (U+2026) not three ASCII periods; verify the test assertion matches. NEVER recommend descoping; propose inline-fix or file as TF-020P-NNN. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness.json.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness-package.json:12:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/input.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness-package.json:13:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness-package.json:14:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/deny/input.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-correctness-package.json:15:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/deny/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"COMPLETENESS/GOVERNANCE R1 review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020P at branch feature/wp-scp-022-020p-scp-r-004-implementation, HEAD commit `9f8639d`. Verify (1) all DISPATCH-NOTE.md slice acceptance items (i)-(xiv) are met or explicitly deferred to subsequent steps; (2) all RULE-001 §9 Phase-2 deliverables are landed; (3) the RFC-process governance changes (TF-020L-002 + TF-020L-003 closures) actually solve the framework gaps as documented in the original TF entries. Specifically: (1) DISPATCH-NOTE acceptance criteria (i)-(xiv): walk each criterion against the actual file changes. (i) policies/SCP-R-004.rego — verify own predicates + sprintf message format + scp_r_004_truncate_reason. (ii) policies/tests/scp_r_004_test.rego — verify ~14 cases (actually 19 cases per the implementation; that's MORE thorough than spec, OK). (iii) tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/{allow,deny}/ — verify both fixtures + expected-verdict.json. (iv) Conflict-gate adapter wiring — verify _evaluate_scp_r_004_python + dispatch in _run_python_audit. (v) Workflow warn-baseline rendering — verify WARN_BASELINE_RULES set + warn-baseline branch + exclusion from effective_denies. (vi) version-manifest.json bump — verify 1.0.1 → 1.1.0. (vii) Release notes — verify docs/releases/v1.1.0.md exists + covers required sections. (viii) ADOPT-001 §12.7.14 — verify subsection added with 3 response options + meta-waiver shape. (ix) RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 amendment — verify residual-bypass guidance. (x) README.md §3 amendment — verify 'new domain' definition. (xi) Adversarial review — verify lens packages + result JSONs are committed (R1 is being dispatched right now via this package; verify the package files exist + are well-formed JSON). (xii) PR open + operator-merge — deferred to next step. (xiii) Tag-cut v1.1.0 — deferred to operator-driven step. (xiv) STATUS.md backfill — deferred to close-out commit. (2) RULE-001 §9 'ADOPT-001 maintenance' deliverable: read RULE-001 §9 then verify §12.7.14 in ADOPT-001 actually addresses the named adopter response options (gradual amendment / rule-config disable / bulk cleanup). (3) Workflow integration — RULE-001 §3.4 'Warn-baseline workflow integration' paragraph specified the workflow needs to recognise SCP-R-004 deny output as warning-class. Verify the implementation matches. (4) The ~12 Conflict-gate fixtures named in RULE-001 §6.2: the implementation has 2 fixtures (1 allow + 1 deny), not 12. Is this a completeness gap? Read RULE-001 §6.2 to understand the spec — the §6.2 corpus is the planned exhaustive set; the implementation chose to put 12 cases in the Conftest tests (which exhaustively cover the corpus) and keep the conflict-gate fixtures minimal (smoke-test for engine agreement, matching SCP-R-001/002 pattern). Is this reasonable, OR should the conflict-gate corpus be expanded to match RULE-001 §6.2 verbatim? (5) The DISPATCH-NOTE 'Out of scope' decisions — TF-020N-001, TF-020H4-001, TF-020H3rg-004, TF-020H4-003 explicitly NOT bundled. Verify the reasoning is consistent (different concerns / different workflows). (6) The release notes claim 'No new D-NNN row in this release'. Verify this is correct — SCP-R-004 is same-domain as SCP-R-002 (per RULE-001 §9 + the README §3 'new domain' definition added in this slice), so no merge-time D-NNN required. (7) TF-020L-002 closure: verify RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 amendment text matches the closure path documented in FIX-ROUND-2.md (slice 020L). The closure path was: 'amend RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 implicit-exclusion-set guidance with a note pointing at RULE-001 §5 case 5 as worked example'. Verify the amendment fulfills this. (8) TF-020L-003 closure: verify README.md §3 'new domain' definition matches the closure path: 'amend README.md §3 Merge to define new domain (new top-level input schema OR no existing SCP-R-NNN counterpart). Same-input-schema additions are same-domain.' Verify the amendment fulfills this. (9) Naming convention check: per slice 020L README §Process step 1 amendment, RULE-NNN.md is the proposal file name; SCP-R-NNN is the rule implementation sequence. Slice 020P's filename is policies/SCP-R-004.rego — verify this matches the naming convention (uppercase + dashes + .rego, mirroring SCP-R-001/002/003). (10) Cross-cutting completeness: does the slice introduce any documentation that contradicts existing documentation? E.g. does §12.7.14 say something about waiver shape that contradicts §12.7.7 or RULE-001 §3.3? (11) Process gaps from THIS slice (file as TF-020P-NNN if found): is the hardcoded WARN_BASELINE_RULES set the right approach long-term, or does the slice need a forward-looking note about the data-driven manifest? (DISPATCH-NOTE flags this as TF-020P-001 candidate — verify this forward-look is sufficient.) (12) Renovate/v* preset implications: does v1.1.0 require any update to the Renovate preset configuration (e.g. is renovate/v1.0.0 the preset's correct cascade target, or should a renovate/v1.1.0 preset be cut?). NEVER recommend descoping; propose inline-fix or file as TF-020P-NNN. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness.json.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness-package.json:8:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/input.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness-package.json:9:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness-package.json:10:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/deny/input.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/review-completeness-package.json:11:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/deny/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:24:| `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/{allow,deny}/` (fixtures) | **IN** | 1 allow + 1 deny, matching SCP-R-001/002 pattern. |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:51:- The conflict-gate fixtures follow `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-002/{allow,deny}/` shape exactly.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:64:- [ ] **(iii) Conflict-gate fixtures.** `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/{input.json, expected-verdict.json}` (single waiver with URL — should not fire) + `.../deny/{input.json, expected-verdict.json}` (single waiver without URL — should fire).\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:98:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/allow/{input.json, expected-verdict.json}` (NEW).\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020p/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:99:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/deny/{input.json, expected-verdict.json}` (NEW).\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/codex-core-audit.log:158:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' docs/DECISIONS.md && printf '\\\\\\\\n---STATUS---\\\\\\\\n' && sed -n '1,360p' STATUS.md && printf '\\\\\\\\n---PLAN---\\\\\\\\n' && sed -n '1,280p' docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Standards Control Plane — Decision Log\\n\\n**Last Updated:** 2026-05-03 (D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A; estate cascade)\\n\\n> **D-021 reservation note (2026-04-28):** D-021 is reserved for the\\n> 2026-05-31 atomic workday filing per WP-SCP-019 hygiene response (PR\\n> #30, 2026-04-21). Codex executors running WP-SCP-022 implementation\\n> slices must NOT assign D-021 to any decision filed during the\\n> autonomous run. The WP-SCP-022 §4.7 gate-enforcement helper\\n> (`scripts/wp_scp_022_gate_check.sh --check-d021`) detects collisions.\\n\\n> **D-041 / D-042 / D-043 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 023A):**\\n> D-041 is reserved for WP-SCP-023 slice 023B (cross-repo scorecard data\\n> shape + opt-in adopter participation model). D-042 is reserved for\\n> slice 023C (aggregator pipeline trust model — GitHub Actions Artifact\\n> Attestation for adopter emits + SCP-side Ed25519 key registry).\\n> **Mandatory trust constraint on D-042 (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001,\\n> per WP-SCP-023 §5 step 3):** the 023C aggregator MUST verify the\\n> `job_workflow_ref` OIDC claim in each artifact attestation against the\\n> SCP reusable workflow path AND SHA pin via `gh attestation verify\\n> --signer-workflow <owner>/<repo>/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@<sha>`;\\n> OIDC JWKS signature verification alone is not sufficient.\\n> D-043 is reserved for slice 023D (MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard`\\n> shape + read-only contract). Codex executors dispatched for any\\n> WP-SCP-023 implementation slice must NOT assign D-041, D-042, or D-043\\n> to any other decision filed during that slice. The reservation pattern\\n> mirrors the D-021 guard above.\\n\\n> **D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 024A):**\\n> D-044 is reserved for WP-SCP-024 slice 024B (`scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh`\\n> = `SCP-073-scaffolder` operational contract + adopter-template versioning\\n> + invocation-log convention extension + `enable-required-check.sh\\n> --restore` rollback mode). D-045 is reserved for slice 024C (estate\\n> cascade ordering canary-first per WP-SCP-024 §5.1, per-adopter\\n> onboarding contract per §5.2, post-bake observation window ≥1 calendar\\n> week + ≥1 Renovate bump cycle per invariant 8, cascade-slice rollback\\n> procedure per invariant 7). D-046 is reserved for slice 024G\\n> (estate-cascade Threshold A criteria + post-Threshold-A maintenance\\n> posture: recurring drift remediation, version-ramp coordination,\\n> departing-adopter procedure; decision on whether `scp.consult_estate_status`\\n> ships standalone or rolls into existing `consult_scorecard`). Codex\\n> executors dispatched for any WP-SCP-024 implementation slice must NOT\\n> assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that\\n> slice. The reservation pattern mirrors the D-021 / D-041–043 guards\\n> above.\\n\\n| ID | Date | Decision | Status | Rationale |\\n|----|------|----------|--------|-----------|\\n| D-001 | 2026-04-11 | Use Python for the phase 1 CLI-first implementation | ACCEPTED | Fast path to explicit schemas, local tooling, JSON validation, and deterministic file-based execution |\\n| D-002 | 2026-04-11 | Keep the project as a standalone app rather than extending docs-agent or Control Tower directly | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator runtime independent while still allowing later integration with both systems |\\n| D-003 | 2026-04-11 | Limit the first governed implementation slice to standards registry loading, minimal findings loading, and consult retrieval | ACCEPTED | Keeps the first end-to-end work package narrow enough to review rigorously while still allowing real consult output |\\n| D-004 | 2026-04-11 | Introduce an explicit project-area intermediate representation before evaluator implementation | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator inputs deterministic, testable, and independent from raw repo sprawl |\\n| D-005 | 2026-04-11 | Wire governance evaluation through the audit CLI before attempting architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | Establishes one real evaluator path end to end before widening the audit surface |\\n| D-006 | 2026-04-11 | Extend audit and project-area contracts before WP-SCP-003 implementation | ACCEPTED | Governance review evidence and unsupported-domain coverage must be first-class structured data, not implied by prose or raw path scanning |\\n| D-007 | 2026-04-11 | Use an explicit unattended-delivery protocol for the remaining programme | ACCEPTED | The remaining work should continue without waiting for user answers, using documented default decisions, bounded review loops, and backlog carry-forward for non-blocking residuals |\\n| D-008 | 2026-04-12 | Use explicit file-content signal heuristics for phase-1 architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | The architecture slice needs deterministic, test-backed checks against repo-local evidence rather than broad semantic inference or prompt-only judgement |\\n| D-009 | 2026-04-12 | Add a second repo-backed fixture corpus for architecture signal coverage | ACCEPTED | `ARCH-003` and `ARCH-004` need concrete repo-backed sample files instead of synthetic payload-only tests if the slice is going to prove its own honesty |\\n| D-010 | 2026-04-12 | Reconcile findings by deterministic finding ID and area-scoped audit coverage | ACCEPTED | Findings persistence must refresh and resolve records only inside the audited area and evaluated domains so unrelated history is not disturbed |\\n| D-011 | 2026-04-12 | Guard findings persistence with scope-collision checks and per-file atomic replacement plus rollback | ACCEPTED | Persisted lifecycle state must fail on `finding_id` scope collisions and should avoid half-written files without pretending phase 1 has a crash-safe multi-file transaction |\\n| D-012 | 2026-04-12 | Bind generated report and area-summary timestamps to the current audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | Report outputs need byte-stable reruns and freshness checks, so they should reuse `audit_result.generated_at` rather than inventing wall-clock timestamps |\\n| D-013 | 2026-04-12 | Reuse the current write-flow guarantees for report artifacts and carry true bundled transactions to backlog | ACCEPTED | The reporting slice should stay downstream of the existing persistence guarantees instead of pretending to offer a crash-safe cross-artifact commit in phase 1 |\\n| D-014 | 2026-04-12 | Guard `report` freshness with embedded findings-state signatures as well as audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | A date-bound audit timestamp alone is too weak to detect same-day content drift, so the report needs a stronger freshness check against persisted findings state |\\n| D-015 | 2026-04-12 | Apply waivers as an audit-time overlay rather than embedding waiver rules inside evaluators | ACCEPTED | Evaluators should keep emitting deterministic raw findings so the same checks can be reused with or without waivers, while audit assembly owns exception handling |\\n| D-016 | 2026-04-12 | Centralise score calculation in a shared module and count only active unwaived findings against score | ACCEPTED | Score semantics must be documented, deterministic, and consistent across evaluators and audit assembly, and waived issues should not drag active score down |\\n| D-017 | 2026-04-12 | Use one embedded fenced JSON review-evidence block as the shared contract for governance validation and historical review retrieval | ACCEPTED | One small structured block inside markdown keeps review evidence inspectable in git while avoiding two drifting review-data formats |\\n| D-018 | 2026-04-12 | Keep historical review retrieval as explicit consult output rather than hiding it inside generic guidance text | ACCEPTED | Review history should be visible, bounded, and machine-readable so later consumers can use it without reparsing prose |\\n| D-019 | 2026-04-18 | SVC-003 is declare-a-contract with a closed four-mode set (`mode.user_oidc`, `mode.service_rs256`, `mode.api_key`, `mode.bearer_legacy`); commit `66ba8a4` codifies `mode.user_oidc` with no rework; WP-SCP-018 `--auth-token` classifies as `mode.bearer_legacy` with deprecation close date 2026-06-30 | ACCEPTED | Estate reality has three live token types plus a cookie session flow; a single fixed mechanism breaks machine callers; a closed mode set prevents drift; codifying the existing CT OIDC layer keeps the in-repo auth story consistent without forcing a rework |\\n| D-020 | 2026-04-18 | `_normalise_scope` accepts `request.scope.area_id` as the normaliser hint when `_infer_area_id` raises `AreaIdInferenceError`; mismatched-inference still raises the same way as before | ACCEPTED | Adopters auditing scopes that lack an ENH-style marker (e.g. a root-level `services.yml` dogfood) would otherwise have to plant placeholder enhancement docs to satisfy area-id inference; the narrow relaxation preserves the auditor discipline of catching inferred-vs-claimed mismatches while closing the genuine uninferrable-scope ergonomic gap |\\n| D-022 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt reusable GitHub Actions workflow + OPA/Conftest + Renovate shared preset + required status check with `enforce_admins=true` as the estate's policy federation primitive. Alternatives rejected: `palantir/policy-bot` (GitHub App per org, HCL-not-Rego, no Renovate cascade, no native waiver-overlay); custom control-plane service (build cost, duplicates GH Actions + branch protection primitives) | ACCEPTED | Industry-standard shape; no custom control-plane service required; cheapest path converting SCP from advisory auditor to pre-merge gate; Renovate preset cascade gives \\\"change one rule → enforce across 10 repos in 24h\\\"; supports waiver-aware evaluation and structured annotation payloads. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 |\\n| D-023 | 2026-04-21 | Reject multi-agent free-form chat-forum pattern as the agent-coordination venue; adopt structured proposal queue (`docs/reviews/proposals/PROP-NNN.md` + MCP `propose()` tool — initial stub in WP-SCP-021 slice 021E; full adjudication workflow in `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue`, separately tracked per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §12). Agents never free-form chat with each other; they read SCP's decision records and call SCP's MCP tools | ACCEPTED | Du et al. (ICML 2024, `arxiv 2305.14325`) show multi-agent debate converges to consensus fast including when the consensus is wrong; Anthropic's multi-agent research post reports ~15× token cost with compounding coordination errors; human-on-the-loop literature converges on moderator-as-bottleneck past a few decisions/hour. Proposal-queue pattern scales because the human is on the loop, not in it. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 (decision originator) and `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 (`propose()` stub design). Note 2026-04-28: original D-023 forward-referenced \\\"WP-SCP-022 plan when that WP opens\\\"; WP-SCP-022 v0.2 is the implementation programme plan, not the proposal-queue WP — the rationale-body expansion lives in the parent plans named here, and the adjudication WP is renamed `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue` |\\n| D-024 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt Anthropic's Model Context Protocol (MCP) as the estate's agent-to-SCP tool protocol. Transports: stdio (primary, local, trust-the-process) + streamable HTTP (opt-in, bearer-token via `SCP_MCP_TOKEN` with 30-day rotation overlap). Ed25519 asymmetric signing for consult receipts — SCP holds private key; adopters verify with public key published at `scp://security/signing-keys` + committed `docs/security/mcp-signing-keys.pub` (90-day rotation overlap). Python `mcp` SDK pinned by version + sdist SHA256; distributed via PyPI as `standards-control-plane[mcp]` extra. OAuth deferred to SCP-075-oauth | ACCEPTED | MCP is the dominant vertical agent-to-tool protocol post Linux Foundation Agentic AI Foundation launch (Dec 2025); Anthropic is protocol origin; Claude Code/Desktop + ACC speak MCP natively. Asymmetric signing prevents adopter-held-key forgery (HMAC symmetric rejected in adversarial review round 1). Alternatives rejected: raw REST (no client ecosystem); custom JSON-RPC (reinvents MCP); gRPC (no agent client support); HMAC symmetric (forgeable by any key-holding adopter). Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\\n| D-025 | 2026-04-21 | Scoped pre-code consult requirement with Ed25519-signed receipts. Downstream `CLAUDE.md` mandates `scp.consult_rules` before code authoring in governed domains; Claude Code `PreCommit` hook refuses commit without valid receipt. Scope rule: hook consults `scp://rules/domain-map` via `resolve_domain(changed_files)`; non-empty result → receipt required; default-skip globs for docs-only changes. Receipt envelope binds `{schema_version, key_id, repo, head_sha, base_sha, changed_files_hash, domains_covered, issued_at, expires_at ≤ 2h}`; hook validates every field. Break-glass `SCP_MCP_ALLOW_OFFLINE=true` requires committed `docs/overrides/OVERRIDE-NNN.md` with schema-validated expiry ≤ 14d; emits tracked finding SCP-MCP-E010 | ACCEPTED | Client-side enforcement pairs with server-side gate (WP-SCP-020) as belt + braces; scoped + TTL + PR-bound receipts prevent replay/forgery/scope-bypass. Break-glass requires sibling OVERRIDE artefact so fail-closed posture is maintained. Rego gate is authoritative — MCP consult never bypasses. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\\n| D-026 | 2026-04-28 | Autonomous-dispatch protocol for SCP implementation slices. Each WP-SCP-020 and WP-SCP-021 implementation slice flows through four-tier dispatch: Opus orchestrator (current agent) prepares dispatch package conforming to `~/Projects/acc/schemas/codex_work_package.schema.json`; Codex executor tier runs the slice via `codex_dispatch.py`; 3× parallel Sonnet R1 reviewers apply correctness / safety_bypass / completeness_governance lenses via `claude_dispatch.py` with 500 ms stagger; Opus consolidates verdicts and orchestrates fix rounds. Recurse to fixpoint per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`. Fix-round budget 5; on budget exhaustion, slice escalates to user review. Evidence persists at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/<slice-id>/`. No slice merges without 3× APPROVED verdict | ACCEPTED | Estate four-tier dispatch standard since 2026-04-22 (per `feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md`); applies to non-trivial work. SCP implementation slices are non-trivial — they touch CI, branch protection, signing keys, and PyPI. Adversarial review pattern proven on WP-SCP-019 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-020 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-021 (3 rounds, fixpoint). Persisting evidence in-repo gives a permanent audit trail and lets future agents recover from session-bounded amnesia. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4 |\\n| D-027 | 2026-04-28 | Parallel-track execution for WP-SCP-020 (federation primitive) and WP-SCP-021 (MCP server) implementation, Scope Y per user 2026-04-28 selection. Both tracks run concurrently from dispatch-zero to user-gate. Track 1 (020): 020B → 020B.1 → 020B.2 → 020C → 020C.1 → 020J → 020K → 020D1 → 020H part 1 → 020E.a → [USER-GATE-A0 release sign-off] → 020H part 2 → 020D2 → [USER-GATE-A] (12 implementation slices + 1 mid-chain human-input gate + 1 terminal gate, matching WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical sequence verbatim). Track 2 (021): 021B → 021C → 021D → 021E → [USER-GATE-C] (4 