lidates verdicts and orchestrates fix rounds. Recurse to fixpoint per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`. Fix-round budget 5; on budget exhaustion, slice escalates to user review. Evidence persists at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/<slice-id>/`. No slice merges without 3× APPROVED verdict | ACCEPTED | Estate four-tier dispatch standard since 2026-04-22 (per `feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md`); applies to non-trivial work. SCP implementation slices are non-trivial — they touch CI, branch protection, signing keys, and PyPI. Adversarial review pattern proven on WP-SCP-019 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-020 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-021 (3 rounds, fixpoint). Persisting evidence in-repo gives a permanent audit trail and lets future agents recover from session-bounded amnesia. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4 |\\n| D-027 | 2026-04-28 | Parallel-track execution for WP-SCP-020 (federation primitive) and WP-SCP-021 (MCP server) implementation, Scope Y per user 2026-04-28 selection. Both tracks run concurrently from dispatch-zero to user-gate. Track 1 (020): 020B → 020B.1 → 020B.2 → 020C → 020C.1 → 020J → 020K → 020D1 → 020H part 1 → 020E.a → [USER-GATE-A0 release sign-off] → 020H part 2 → 020D2 → [USER-GATE-A] (12 implementation slices + 1 mid-chain human-input gate + 1 terminal gate, matching WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical sequence verbatim). Track 2 (021): 021B → 021C → 021D → 021E → [USER-GATE-C] (4 implementation slices + 1 terminal gate). Concurrent-dispatch limit: 4 active Codex sessions across both tracks. `pyproject.toml` partition: Track 1 owns `[tool.scp.federation]` and adds `scp-policy-check` to `[project.scripts]`; Track 2 owns `[project.optional-dependencies.mcp]` and adds `scp-mcp-server` to `[project.scripts]`. `docs/STATUS.md` excluded from all slice scope_boundary globs | ACCEPTED | WP-SCP-021 §scope statement declares the tracks independent; serial execution would defer MCP availability for ~2× wall-clock without quality gain. Concurrent dispatch within rate-limit envelope per `four-tier-dispatch-field-report-2026-04-22.md`. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.4. Track 1 sequence corrected in v0.2 (R1 fix-round) to match WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical ordering — earlier draft omitted 020K and inverted 020D2 placement |\\n| D-028 | 2026-04-28 | SCP adopts D-048 / ADR-016 Design Parity Build Method (DPBM) as a contract input for any SCP slice that produces designed visual output. Layered amendment to four-tier dispatch — prototype + flows + design principles enter the contract layer the build is bound to. Enforcement-injection mechanism per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6: visual-output slices' dispatch packages MUST include a \\\"DPBM contract\\\" subsection naming prototype path, flow diagram, and design-principles reference; orchestrator verifies subsection presence before launching the review pass; absence is treated as a CRIT finding. Within WP-SCP-022 itself no slices match the DPBM \\\"applies\\\" criteria (workflow / Rego / MCP-server / CI work, no visual output); D-028 is forward-looking. WP-SCP-024 estate cascade inherits D-028; FLA, ACC, RI canary-onboarding instructions reference the DPBM contract layer. D-028 lands operationally once CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 have merged | ACCEPTED | DPBM closes a structural gap that pure-code lenses can't see — a frontend built under standard four-tier dispatch can satisfy every test and still drift from the design. Without an SCP-side adoption decision, agents working through SCP's MCP consult won't be told that the contract for visual-output work includes design artefacts. CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 (both 2026-04-28) introduce the upstream doctrine; SCP adopts at the policy layer. Full rationale + injection-mechanism specification in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6 |\\n| D-029 | 2026-04-29 | `policy-check.yml` reusable workflow `permissions:` block expands to `contents: read, statuses: write` to support WP-SCP-022 020C.1(vi) read-back commit-status posting on the sibling context `scp/policy-check-readback`. Original 020B(iii) baseline was `contents: read` only. The `statuses: write` privilege uses the caller's `${{ github.token }}` and posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`; no other write surface is unlocked. Disabling the read-back would require accepting a regression on 020C.1(vi); keeping `contents: read` only would prevent the read-back from being posted | ACCEPTED | The read-back surface is the only place a PR reviewer sees waiver/disabled-rule posture without reading JSON artefacts; WP-SCP-020 §4.3 fixpoint criteria for 020C.1(vi) require it. The privilege footprint is narrowest possible — `statuses: write` does not allow content modification, branch operations, issue/PR comments, or token impersonation. The workflow checkout uses `persist-credentials: false` so the elevated privilege is bounded to the policy-check job. Plan amendment: `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020B(iii) updated with cross-reference to D-029. Identified by WP-SCP-022 R1 completeness review (MAJ-005) |\\n| D-030 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020J on the SCP repository: (a) tag-protection rule on `v*` (legacy tag-protection API and/or Repository Ruleset) preventing force-push or deletion of release tags; (b) `required-signed-commits` on `main` so every commit reaching `main` carries a verified signature. Both are preconditions for slice 020D1 (self-dogfood wrapper pinned at the v1.0.0-rc.1 commit-SHA) and 020D2 (required-status-check + v1.0.0 cut). Configured via `scripts/configure-020j-protections.sh` (idempotent API caller). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md`. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) can disable these via repo Settings; quarterly review cadence per WP-SCP-020 §14 U-sec-2 closure | ACCEPTED | Path (a) selected at WP-S