w warns on post-v1.2.0 SHAs while keeping the v1.0.0 pin as the operational fallback until TF-023E-002 closes. **Cascade-status spec hardened over 9 review rounds** (32 unique MAJ + 1 CRIT closed across 8 fix-rounds — unprecedented; cause was the cascade-status / CI-enforcement clause being structurally cross-cutting): 3 close-states (`onboarded`, `onboarded-operator-bump`, `blocked-on-adopter-conflict`) × 4 CI behaviours (3 named + fail-closed default) × (positive evidence + machine-enforceable regex format spec + negative assertion). WP-SCP-024 is the natural successor to the 5-move MVCP — turns SCP's estate observability (WP-SCP-023) into action. Merged at `4dc3faa`. |\\n123:**Operator decision (locked 2026-05-09):** the 6 D-035-aligned rules previously framed as gating CT Wave 1 entry on 2026-05-15 are **RETIRED under that deadline** — re-scoped under WP-SCP-025 (parked, post-WP-SCP-024 Threshold A). The retirement is conditional on a strategic reframe: SCP's near-term credibility depends on shipping 2-3 high-value domain rules that catch things review misses + measurable adopter consumption (≥3 cohort adopters running ≥2 SCP-R-005+ rules with ≥80% pass rate over ≥4-week observation window), not on forcing the 6 SDK-discipline rules through under deadline pressure when (a) CT D-035 Phase 5 already shipped 2026-05-07 without these rules as preconditions; (b) WP-SCP-024 cascade is operator-decided FLA-independent per 2026-05-03 §2 invariants row 4; (c) none of the 6 had been drafted with 6 days remaining; (d) parallel work on slice 024B (the actual cascade gate per plan-doc §6) would have been diluted.\\n137:**Slice 024B-core remains the immediate work** — hard predecessor for cohort onboarding 024C-F per WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §6. Scope: scaffolder + adopter wrapper template + `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` + tests; 024B-extras deferred.\\n141:**Operator decision (locked 2026-05-09):** WP-SCP-024 slice 024B split into 024B-core (scaffolder + adopter wrapper template + cascade-status CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (`--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Empirical justification: original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code; R6 missed a primary deliverable; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR. Surface-area-too-large for single-pass convergence. Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope — both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens.\\n167:| WP-SCP-023 Threshold A operator sign-off — `docs/gates/USER-GATE-D.md` lands as `not-yet-signed` at slice 023E; signs when ≥3 adopters opted in + ≥1 weekly aggregator clean. Operator-driven; no fixed date. | tbd | docs/gates/USER-GATE-D.md |\\n172:- ~~**TF-006**~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020Q** (this PR) — `tests/conflict_gate/test_conflict_gate.py` gains Python-side mirrors of `policies/scp_common.rego` suppression helpers (`_scp_active_waiver_for`, `_scp_waiver_expired`, `_scp_rule_config_disabled`, `_parse_dateish_ns`); `_run_opa` detects sibling `waivers.json` + `.scp/rule-config.yaml` and passes via `--data`; 5 new fixtures land — SCP-R-001/{waiver-suppressed, waiver-expired, rule-config-disabled} + SCP-R-002/waiver-suppressed + SCP-R-004/waiver-suppressed. All 11 fixtures pass; both engines agree on every code path. Finding-id-level matching deferred to WP-SCP-023 per scp_common.rego comment. Closes the WP-SCP-023 prerequisite gate.\\n187:- **TF-020H3-004**: §12.7.4 path examples (`services.yml`, `output/findings/waivers.json`, `policies/**`) are SCP-internal naming (was R1 completeness COMP-NIT-001). Estate-cascade adopters (FLA, PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower per WP-SCP-024) may have different file-shape conventions; v1.1 §12.7 wording could generalise. No deadline (cosmetic).\\n193:- **TF-020H1-002**: `canary-replay.yml` paging rotation — at v1.0.0 the auto-opened `rule-regression` issue assigns `@jrnb2024` only (single-operator per D-031). For estate cascade (WP-SCP-024) a paging rotation would replace the assignee-only signal. Closure path: revisit at WP-SCP-024 planning OR at the 2026-07-21 quarterly bus-factor review when a second maintainer onboards. No deadline (forward-compat). Filed at 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MAJ-002 closure.\\n194:- **TF-020H1-003**: `auto-defer` GitHub Action for stale rule-proposals — currently a manual close + `defer` label step (per `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2). For estate scale (post-WP-SCP-024 cascade) when proposal volume warrants automation, a scheduled GH Action would close PRs with no approvals after the 48h window. No deadline (forward-compat). Filed at 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MIN-004 closure.\\n230:## Tracked-forward items from WP-SCP-023 023A (plan-doc) + 023B (emitter) + 023C (aggregator)\\n232:- **TF-023A-001** (low priority): R-023-01 k-anonymity mitigation framing clarification when WP-SCP-024 cascade slice begins. The plan-doc R-023-01 risk row references \\\"slice 024 introduces aggregator-side k-anonymity\\\"; tighten when WP-SCP-024 plan-doc lands. Forward-compat; opportunistic.\\n233:- **TF-023A-002** (medium priority): Cross-repo notification (v1.2.0 release announcement to ACC / CT / mapp-estate-regression) — **re-deferred to slice 023E** (was 023D; 023D ships exposure surface but Threshold A onboarding is where adopters first participate). Until adopters can opt in with the exposure surface live, the notification is a \\\"pre-Threshold-A FYI\\\" only. Filed at WP-SCP-023 023A; re-deferred at 023D R1 completeness CMP-MIN-001.\\n250:- **TF-023E-002** (medium priority): SCP-self wrapper SHA pin is **stuck at v1.0.0 (@41a5299)** — bumping to any post-WP-SCP-023 SHA produces `startup_failure` because GitHub Actions validates declared permissions on ALL jobs in a called workflow at startup (BEFORE `if:` evaluation) against the caller's ceiling. The `attest-scorecard` job in `policy-check.yml@5ff2acd` declares `attestations: write` + `id-token: write`, which exceeds the wrapper's `contents:read + statuses:write` ceiling, even when `scorecard-emit: false` would skip the job at runtime. Closure path: (a) restructure `policy-check.yml` so `attest-scorecard` lives in a separate top-level workflow file (called workflows can't have job-scoped perms above caller ceiling regardless of `if:`), OR (b) once TF-023E-001 unblocks (repo public/org-owned), grant `attestations: write` + `id-token: write` universally at the wrapper level. (a) preserves least-privilege for opt-out adopters and is the recommended path. Surfaced at 023E fix-round-3 (the bumped wrapper failed startup_failure even after reverting `scorecard-emit: true` because the SHA bump alone trips the validation). Real adopters that opt in MUST follow the (b) path because they will universally grant the two permissions per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2.\\n258:## Recent decisions\\n272:- **D-041 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B. MAJOR-pinned schema; default-false `scorecard-emit: bool` workflow_call input; OIDC artifact attestation via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0`; aggregator verifies at slice 023C via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow`. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema's `additionalProperties: false`). v1.1.0 → v1.2.0 (MINOR per VERSIONING.md additive-opt-in). Closes plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation.\\n273:- **D-042 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C. Per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (mandatory `job_workflow_ref` constraint pre-committed at 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits + CODEOWNERS + branch protection (NOT a separate Ed25519 layer; Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub). Two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) mirrors 023B's `attest-scorecard` job pattern. Internal-only; no semver bump. Closes plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation.\\n274:- **D-043 (2026-05-03)** — Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D. Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` (deterministic golden-file-tested). MCP method has read-only contract per plan-doc invariant 5 (request: `repo_filter?` + `since_emitted_at?`; response: aggregated counts only, never waiver content per invariant 2; Pydantic-enforced field whitelist). ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. Closes plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation.\\n293:- **WP-SCP-023**: cross-repo scorecards.\\n294:- **WP-SCP-024**: estate cascade (FLA pilot → PIM/recommender/etc.).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"SAFETY/BYPASS R1 review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020Q at branch feature/wp-scp-022-020q-conflict-gate-suppression-corpus, HEAD commit 05cab6d. The slice extends conflict-gate fixture coverage to suppression scenarios. Verify SAFETY surface exhaustively. Specifically: (1) Bypass-surface inventory: could the new helpers or fixtures create a path that silently allows what should deny? Concrete attack paths: (a) Could a fixture's waivers.json contain something that suppresses the WRONG rule (different rule_id) and silently make Python pass while Rego catches it (or vice versa)? — verify _scp_active_waiver_for matches rule_id strictly. (b) Could a malformed expires_at silently appear unexpired in Python while Rego correctly flags as expired? — verify _scp_waiver_expired's three branches (missing/empty/unparseable/<=now). (c) Could Python's _parse_dateish_ns accept a date format Rego rejects (or vice versa), producing engine divergence? — specifically check timezone handling, leap-year edge cases, and the 'Z' → '+00:00' normalisation. (d) Could _scp_rule_config_disabled silently treat truthy non-True values (1, 'true', etc.) as True? — verify the Python `is True` strict check. (e) Could _build_opa_data_arg's tempfile race with another process or be observed by other users? — verify tempfile.mkstemp creates with mode 0600 (Python default), and the path is unlinked promptly. (2) Path-traversal / symlink-following: could a malicious fixture's waivers.json sibling path escape the scenario directory via symlink? — _load_sibling_waivers / _load_sibling_rule_config use `scenario_dir / 'waivers.json'` and `scenario_dir / '.scp' / 'rule-config.yaml'` — Path-relative; symlink resolution would follow but the file would still be under the test corpus. Acceptable for test code; flag if otherwise. (3) yaml.safe_load vs yaml.load — verify all yaml usage is safe_load (no RCE via yaml deserialization). (4) Tempfile cleanup under exception — verify the try/finally in _run_opa unlinks the tempfile even when subprocess.run raises (e.g. timeout). Verify _build_opa_data_arg unlinks on write exception. (5) JSON injection via fixture — could a maliciously crafted fixture file contain shell metacharacters that escape into the opa command line? — verify all paths are passed as proper subprocess.run arg-list elements (not joined into a shell string), so shell quoting concerns don't apply. (6) Concurrency — could two parallel test runs collide on tempfile names? — tempfile.mkstemp returns process-unique names; verify. (7) .gitignore negation rule — could the new `!tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/` rule accidentally un-ignore production .scp files elsewhere? — the negation is bounded to tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**, but verify by reading the rule + thinking about edge cases (e.g. could a future repo restructure inadvertently move .scp under tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/ and leak production rule-config?). Low-probability but flag. (8) CODEOWNERS coverage — verify `tests/** @jrnb2024` (line 36) covers the new fixture files; `docs/integrations/** @jrnb2024` covers the doc edit; existing rules cover .gitignore, STATUS.md. No new entry required. (9) Rego/Python parity gap — could a new fixture be authored that passes both engines but actually misses a corner case (e.g. timezone offset other than UTC)? — flag if the new fixture set has gaps that would let a real production divergence slip through. (10) Threat-model alignment — read SECURITY.md briefly; verify the slice does not violate any documented invariant (no new privileged code paths, no new external network calls in test code). (11) Information disclosure — verify the test code does not log waiver contents to stdout/stderr in CI logs (waivers may carry approver names or URLs that shouldn't appear in public CI logs). (12) New regex `_DATEISH_DATE_RE` and `_DATEISH_DATETIME_RE` — verify they match exactly what scp_common.rego's regex.match patterns accept. Specifically: scp_common.rego uses `^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$` for the date pattern and a more complex pattern for RFC3339 datetime. NEVER recommend descoping; propose inline-fix or file as TF-020Q-NNN. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json conforming to schemas/sonnet_review_result.schema.json.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:8:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-suppressed/waivers.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:9:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-expired/waivers.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:10:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/.scp/rule-config.yaml\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:11:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-002/waiver-suppressed/waivers.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-package.json:12:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/waiver-suppressed/waivers.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-r2.json:15:    \"The R1 self_reported_concern about '!tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/.scp/ (zero intermediate path components)' is now partially mitigated: /.gitignore is CODEOWNERS-gated so any expansion of the negation pattern requires @jrnb2024 review. The edge case itself (a .scp/ dir directly under fixtures/) remains technically possible per gitignore ** semantics but there is no such path in the repo and CODEOWNERS review prevents silent introduction. Retained as a low-probability known edge; no further action warranted at this stage.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety-r2.json:18:  \"summary\": \"R2 safety/bypass review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020Q at HEAD 172ca04 (fix-round-1 applied). All four R1 safety findings verified closed against actual repo state; no new CRIT or MAJ surface introduced by the fix-round-1 deltas. One nit-level new finding (nit-R2-SAFETY-001: clause-4 comment does not document its dependency on clause-1 for missing-key coverage). Bypass surface inventory unchanged from R1 baseline; all three nit-NO-ACTION items carry forward unchanged. Detailed verification: (1) MAJ-SAFETY-001 CLOSED \\u2014 the new 4th scp_waiver_expired clause (policies/scp_common.rego lines 66-69) correctly fires when expires_at is JSON null: `expires_at := w.expires_at` succeeds (key present), `not is_string(null)` is true; fires for all other non-string types (numbers, objects, arrays, booleans) by the same path; does NOT fire on valid strings because `is_string(str)` is true and `not is_string(str)` is false (no regression on existing waivers); missing-key case is cleanly separated: `w.expires_at` is undefined when key absent, the clause body fails, and clause 1 (`object.get(w,'expires_at','') == ''`) handles it via empty-string default \\u2014 no bypass from missing key. The new waiver-null-expires-at fixture (input.yml: mode.invalid_unapproved service; waivers.json: expires_at null; expected-verdict: deny) exercises both engines: Rego fires clause 4 \\u2192 waiver expired \\u2192 not scp_active_waiver_for \\u2192 deny; Python _scp_waiver_expired receives None from dict.get \\u2192 not isinstance(None, str) \\u2192 True \\u2192 expired \\u2192 deny. Both engines agree with expected deny. (2) MIN-SAFETY-002 CLOSED \\u2014 re.ASCII flag confirmed at test_conflict_gate.py lines 53 and 54-57 on both _DATEISH_DATE_RE and _DATEISH_DATETIME_RE; pins \\\\d to [0-9] ASCII-only, matching OPA RE2 semantics. (3) MIN-SAFETY-003 CLOSED \\u2014 CODEOWNERS line 93: `/.gitignore @jrnb2024` inserted; /CODEOWNERS self-protection at line 105 \\u2014 ordering invariant preserved (/.gitignore at 93 < /CODEOWNERS at 105). Comment block at lines 85-92 accurately documents the governance rationale: the 020Q negation rule controls fixture .scp/ tracking; a future broader negation could un-ignore production .scp/ directories; @jrnb2024 review gate closes the surface. CODEOWNERS last-match-wins: no subsequent wildcard pattern covers /.gitignore, so the entry is unambiguously governing. (4) MIN-SAFETY-004 CLOSED \\u2014 rule-config fixture at tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/.scp/rule-config.yaml line 8 uses `justification:` field; schemas/rule-config.schema.json requires `justification` (type string, minLength 1) under patternProperties `^SCP-R-[0-9]{3}$` with additionalProperties false; fixture now schema-conformant. Bypass surface inventory (all unchanged from R1): tampered binary \\u2014 governed by scripts/scp-policy-check.lock; no change. Tampered wrapper \\u2014 _run_opa uses subprocess list not shell=True; no change. Shadow PATH \\u2014 shutil.which('opa') at fixture-scope; test-time only; no change. Shell injection \\u2014 confirmed no shell=True in any subprocess call; no change. Threshold drop \\u2014 no threshold values in new code. Repo-root escape \\u2014 REPO_ROOT/FIXTURES_ROOT/POLICIES_DIR resolved via __file__; no change. Concurrency \\u2014 no shared mutable state introduced. Info disclosure (nit-SAFETY-007) \\u2014 nit-NO-ACTION; unchanged. Downstream consumer null safety: _load_sibling_waivers passes the Python None for null through json.dump \\u2192 serialised back to JSON null \\u2192 OPA receives null in data.waivers; OPA's clause 4 fires correctly. Python _scp_waiver_expired receives None via dict.get and fails-closed. _evaluate_scp_r_002_python would flag a null expires_at as invalid if that waiver were SCP-R-002 input, but waiver-null-expires-at is SCP-R-001-scoped \\u2014 no consumer ambiguity. New clause 4 attack path: the clause exclusively adds to the 'expired' set (fail-closed); no rule fires on 'expired waiver exists'; cannot be crafted to suppress a different rule. CODEOWNERS ordering invariant: /.gitignore at line 93 appears before /CODEOWNERS at line 105 and docs/CODEOWNERS at line 106 \\u2014 invariant preserved. .gitignore negation rule under review: all future .gitignore changes gate through @jrnb2024 review, closing MIN-SAFETY-003's concern about the `!tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/` negation pattern being altered without governance review.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:10:      \"evidence\": \"scp_common.rego line 57: first clause checks expires_at == '' — if expires_at is null, object.get(w, 'expires_at', '') returns null (not the default string), so null == '' is false and the clause does not fire. Lines 60-65: second clause checks is_string(expires_at) — is_string(null) is false, so this clause does not fire. Lines 67-72: third clause also starts with is_string(expires_at) — same result. No default declaration on scp_waiver_expired. OPA rego.v1 semantics: scp_waiver_expired(w) is undefined; not scp_waiver_expired(w) = not undefined = true. scp_active_waiver_for (lines 44-49) then evaluates to true for a matching waiver with null expires_at — deny rule suppressed. Python (test_conflict_gate.py): expires_at = waiver.get('expires_at', '') returns None when the key is present with value null; not isinstance(None, str) = True; returns True (expired). No fixture in tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/waiver-* exercises null expires_at. schemas/waiver.schema.json defines expires_at as type: string (prevents null) but schema validation is a separate layer from policy evaluation.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:12:      \"mitigation\": \"File TF-020Q-001. Fix: add a fourth scp_waiver_expired clause to policies/scp_common.rego that fires when expires_at is present but not a string: `scp_waiver_expired(w) if { expires_at := object.get(w, \\\"expires_at\\\", \\\"\\\"); not is_string(expires_at) }`. This is disjoint from the existing first clause (which fires for the empty-string default when the key is absent) because not is_string(\\\"\\\") = false. Also add a corpus fixture tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-null-expires/ with a waivers.json carrying expires_at: null and expected-verdict.json: deny, to keep both engines honest on this branch.\"\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:25:      \"claim\": \".gitignore (repo root) is not covered by any CODEOWNERS entry. The slice adds a governance-relevant negation rule (!tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/ and !tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/**) that exempts fixture .scp/ directories from the broad .scp/ ignore rule. A future PR modifying .gitignore does not require @jrnb2024 review. An adversary with write access could (a) remove the negation — silently breaking the rule-config-disabled fixture so .scp/ files disappear from tracking, (b) broaden the negation pattern to cover paths outside tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/, potentially un-ignoring production .scp/ rule-config files in other locations.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:26:      \"evidence\": \"CODEOWNERS: reviewed all entries — policies/**, schemas/**, lib/**, .github/**, tests/**, renovate/**, docs/DECISIONS.md, docs/security/**, docs/adoption/**, docs/plans/**, docs/integrations/**, output/findings/**, output/control-tower/**, output/ci/**, scripts/**, vendor/**, version-manifest.json, .scp/**, requirements/**, docs/releases/**, /CODEOWNERS, docs/CODEOWNERS. No entry covers /.gitignore or the root *.gitignore glob. .gitignore line 8: .scp/ (ignores all .scp/ directories). Lines 11-13: !tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/ and !tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/** (negation). The negation is load-bearing for the rule-config-disabled fixture to be tracked by git.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:33:      \"claim\": \"The rule-config-disabled fixture at tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/.scp/rule-config.yaml uses the field name 'reason' but schemas/rule-config.schema.json requires 'justification' with additionalProperties: false. The fixture is schema-invalid. The test passes because _load_sibling_rule_config and scp_rule_config_disabled only check the disable field, not justification. But the fixture is presented as a production-layout reference (DISPATCH-NOTE 'Mirrors production layout (.scp/rule-config.yaml)'); adopters using it as a template would produce non-conformant rule-configs that fail schema validation in the policy-check workflow.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:44:      \"mitigation\": \"File TF-020Q-003 (opportunistic). Add one fixture, e.g. tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-suppressed-tz/ with expires_at: '2099-06-15T12:00:00+05:30' to cover a positive offset and verify both engines agree it is not expired.\"\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:49:      \"claim\": \"_load_sibling_waivers and _load_sibling_rule_config use Path.is_file() and Path.read_text() which both follow symlinks without restriction. A malicious or accidentally mis-created symlink at tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/<rule>/<scenario>/waivers.json pointing outside the corpus would be silently followed and its contents loaded into the OPA data dict or the Python evaluator.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-safety.json:66:    \"The .gitignore negation pattern !tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/**/.scp/ also matches !tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/.scp/ (zero intermediate path components, per gitignore ** semantics). A .scp/ directory placed directly under fixtures/ — not under a rule/scenario subdirectory — would also be un-ignored. This is a very low probability scenario (no such path exists or is planned) but is a subtle edge of the ** pattern.\"\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/FIX-ROUND-3.md:171:4. CODEOWNERS expansion (`docs/gates/**` + `docs/reviews/**` per 023E\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"SAFETY/BYPASS R2 review of WP-SCP-023 slice 023E at branch feature/wp-scp-023-023e-threshold, HEAD commit 648420a (fix-round-1 applied). Read FIX-ROUND-1.md. Verify MAJ-SAFE-001 closure: CODEOWNERS now has `docs/gates/** @jrnb2024` + `docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024` inserted before /CODEOWNERS self-protection per ORDERING INVARIANT. Verify NO new safety surface. NEVER recommend descoping. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2.json.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"CORRECTNESS R2 review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020Q at branch feature/wp-scp-022-020q-conflict-gate-suppression-corpus, HEAD commit 172ca04 (fix-round-1 applied on top of 05cab6d). Read FIX-ROUND-1.md for the disposition table. Verify (1) all R1 findings have an actual repo-state change matching the recorded disposition; (2) NO NEW CRIT/MAJ correctness findings emerge from the fix-round-1 deltas. Specifically: (1) C-COR-MAJ-001 closure: rule-config fixture (`tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/.scp/rule-config.yaml`) now uses `justification:` (not `reason:`) matching `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`. (2) MAJ-SAFETY-001 / C-COR-MIN-001 / COMP-MIN-003 closure: `policies/scp_common.rego` has a 4th `scp_waiver_expired` clause matching `expires_at := w.expires_at; not is_string(expires_at)` — verify the clause is correctly placed (after the existing 3 clauses), syntactically valid Rego v1, and actually covers the null + non-string cases (a waiver with `expires_at: null`, with `expires_at: 12345`, with `expires_at: []`, with `expires_at: {}` — all should now be `expired = true`). (3) C-COR-nit-001 / MIN-SAFETY-002 closure: `_DATEISH_DATE_RE` and `_DATEISH_DATETIME_RE` now compile with `re.ASCII`. (4) C-COR-nit-002 closure: `_load_sibling_waivers` and `_load_sibling_rule_config` now catch `(json.JSONDecodeError, OSError, UnicodeDecodeError)` and `(ImportError, yaml.YAMLError, OSError, UnicodeDecodeError)` respectively, with explicit `encoding='utf-8'`. (5) C-COR-MIN-002 / COMP-MIN-002 closure: `docs/integrations/conflict-gate.md` 'Coverage of the v1.0.0 rule library' table now lists per-rule fixture sets including `waiver-suppressed` / `waiver-expired` / `rule-config-disabled` for SCP-R-001 + SCP-R-004 row added. (6) New fixture `SCP-R-001/waiver-null-expires-at/` exists with input.yml (bad mode), waivers.json (`expires_at: null`), expected-verdict.json (verdict: deny). (7) Pytest still passes 12/12 — verify the test logic. (8) Pre-push wrapper still green at coverage 98.58% (above 90% threshold). (9) Verify NO regression on existing fixtures: SCP-R-001/{allow, deny, waiver-suppressed, waiver-expired, rule-config-disabled}/, SCP-R-002/{allow, deny, waiver-suppressed}/, SCP-R-004/{allow, deny, waiver-suppressed}/ unchanged in behaviour. (10) Verify the new scp_common.rego clause does NOT accidentally make a string `expires_at` resolve to expired (regression on existing fixtures). The new clause uses `not is_string(expires_at)` so strings DO NOT trigger it — verify mentally. (11) Verify FIX-ROUND-1.md is internally consistent. (12) Verify the new fixture's waivers.json carries a URL-bearing `reason` per SCP-R-004 v1.1.0. NEVER recommend descoping; propose inline-fix or file forward. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2.json conforming to schemas/sonnet_review_result.schema.json.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2-package.json:9:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/.scp/rule-config.yaml\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2-package.json:10:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-null-expires-at/input.yml\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2-package.json:11:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-null-expires-at/waivers.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-correctness-r2-package.json:12:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-null-expires-at/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2.json:4:      \"claim\": \"CLOSED. docs/gates/** and docs/reviews/** are now covered in CODEOWNERS with @jrnb2024 review routing. Both rules inserted before /CODEOWNERS self-protection per ORDERING INVARIANT. R1 fix verified.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2.json:5:      \"evidence\": \"CODEOWNERS line 50: 'docs/gates/** @jrnb2024' (comment block lines 43\\u201349 explicitly cites MAJ-SAFE-001 closure). CODEOWNERS line 56: 'docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024' (comment block lines 51\\u201355 cites MAJ-SAFE-001 companion). Both rules appear before the /CODEOWNERS self-protection rule at line 136 (last-match-wins enforced by GitHub). The ORDERING INVARIANT at CODEOWNERS lines 23\\u201331 states that governance-sensitive rules must be added before the /CODEOWNERS block; both additions comply. No broad wildcard follows them. FIX-ROUND-1.md item 3 confirms the edit was deliberate and scoped correctly.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2.json:30:    \"MAJ-SAFE-001 is confirmed closed. Insertion position correct: docs/gates/** at CODEOWNERS line 50 and docs/reviews/** at line 56 both precede /CODEOWNERS at line 136. GitHub last-match-wins means these specific rules are never overridden by a hypothetical future broad wildcard inserted before them. The ORDERING INVARIANT commentary at lines 23\\u201331 is intact and governs future editors.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-023/dispatches/023E-threshold/review-safety-r2.json:36:  \"summary\": \"R2 SAFETY/BYPASS review of WP-SCP-023 023E at HEAD 648420a (fix-round-1 applied). MAJ-SAFE-001 VERIFIED CLOSED: CODEOWNERS now contains docs/gates/** @jrnb2024 at line 50 and docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024 at line 56, both inserted before the /CODEOWNERS self-protection rule at line 136 per ORDERING INVARIANT. No new safety surface introduced by fix-round-1. MIN-SAFE-003 (cross-repo notification) and nit-SAFE-001 (criterion vi ambiguity) are confirmed closed by inline fixes. Two MIN findings carry forward from R1 with unchanged NO ACTION and operational-gate dispositions: MIN-SAFE-001 (IndexError omitted from except clause \\u2014 functionally impossible in realistic deployment) and MIN-SAFE-002 (key ring operationally empty \\u2014 blocked from false closure by USER-GATE-D criterion vi requiring code AND operational satisfaction). No CRIT or MAJ findings remain open. Slice 023E is approved with the two carried-forward MIN findings.\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-completeness-package.json:8:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-suppressed/expected-verdict.json\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/review-completeness-package.json:9:    \"tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-0