t_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-suppressed/` — `input.yml` (bad mode) + `waivers.json` (active SCP-R-001 waiver, far-future expiry) + `expected-verdict.json` (allow) | Primary new corpus entry for waiver suppression. |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:31:| `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-expired/` — `input.yml` (bad mode) + `waivers.json` (waiver with EXPIRED `expires_at`) + `expected-verdict.json` (**deny**) | Fail-closed semantics: expired waiver does NOT suppress. Verifies both Rego and Python apply the same expiry semantics. |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:32:| `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/` — `input.yml` (bad mode) + `.scp/rule-config.yaml` (disable: true for SCP-R-001) + `expected-verdict.json` (allow) | Primary new corpus entry for rule-config disable. Mirrors production layout (`.scp/` directory). |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:33:| `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-002/waiver-suppressed/` — bad waiver-shape input + meta-waiver suppressing SCP-R-002 + expected allow | Coverage for the meta-waiver pattern (RULE-002 §6 case 5 / 020L SAFE-MAJ). |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:34:| `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/waiver-suppressed/` — waiver-missing-URL input + meta-waiver suppressing SCP-R-004 + expected allow | Coverage for SCP-R-004 (warn-baseline) suppression. |\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:60:- The fixture corpus is a small set of JSON/YAML files following an existing pattern (`tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/<rule_id>/{allow,deny}/` already established by 020C.1).\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:116:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-suppressed/{input.yml, waivers.json, expected-verdict.json}`.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:117:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/waiver-expired/{input.yml, waivers.json, expected-verdict.json}`.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:118:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-001/rule-config-disabled/{input.yml, .scp/rule-config.yaml, expected-verdict.json}`.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:119:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-002/waiver-suppressed/{input.json, waivers.json, expected-verdict.json}`.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020q/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:120:- `tests/conflict_gate/fixtures/SCP-R-004/waiver-suppressed/{input.json, waivers.json, expected-verdict.json}`.\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020d1/review-completeness.json:32:    \"result\": \"I have all the context needed. Producing the SonnetReviewResult JSON now.\\n\\n{\\\"package_id\\\":\\\"review-slice-020d1-r1-completeness\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"conditional_pass\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical adopter template uses wrong repo name (no trailing dash), creating adopter copy-paste failure and Renovate pin-bump silent-no-op risk\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical block specifies `uses: jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@<commit-SHA>` and Renovate marker `depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` (no trailing dash). Actual implementation: policy-check-wrapper.yml line 41 uses `jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/\\u2026` (trailing dash present); policy-check.yml line 916 uses `repository: jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` (trailing dash present). Review instruction item (9) independently confirms the correct repo name carries a trailing dash.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"When 020H part 3 publishes the canonical example into ADOPT-001 \\u00a712, any adopter copying from the plan will reference a non-existent GitHub repo path, causing 100% workflow-dispatch failure. The `depName` mismatch also causes Renovate pin bumps to silently no-op, breaking the Renovate-cascade contract that is a core WP-SCP-020 AC.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Before 020H part 3 lands, amend WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical block to `jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` (with trailing dash) in both `uses:` and `depName=`. Not a blocker for 020D1 merge (wrapper itself is correct); tracked-forward to 020H part 3 pre-publication.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Canonical minimal wrapper in WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 omits fork PR refusal `if:` condition; mandatory-vs-optional status is not documented for adopters\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md AC item: 'Wrapper triggers on pull_request to main only (refuses fork PRs via head/base repo equality check).' Wrapper lines 39-40 implement `if: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.event.pull_request.base.repo.full_name }}`. WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical example has no `if:` condition. 020B acceptance criterion (viii-c) implies callers must be same-repo PRs for break-glass gates to apply, but the reusable workflow itself does not hard-enforce same-repo at the workflow level.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Adopters copying the canonical minimal wrapper will not have fork PR refusal. If fork PR refusal is a mandatory AC for all adopters (as the 020D1 slice checklist treats it), the published canonical example is non-compliant and will produce weaker security posture in every downstream adopter. If optional, the gap is undocumented recommended hardening.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Before 020H part 3 lands, explicitly document whether fork PR refusal is mandatory or optional for adopters. If mandatory, add `if:` condition to canonical minimal example. If optional, add it as a named hardening recommendation in ADOPT-001 \\u00a712. Either resolution closes the ambiguity.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Wrapper header comment cites 'ADOPT-001 \\u00a712' as conformance reference but current \\u00a712 content is 'Architecture Principles for Adopters' \\u2014 the federation integration appendix does not yet exist\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"policy-check-wrapper.yml header: 'Wrapper conforms to the canonical adopter shape documented in WP-SCP-020 020H part 3 (ADOPT-001 \\u00a712)'. ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712 (line 716): heading is 'Architecture Principles for Adopters' with subsections 12.1\\u201312.6 covering general extraction/integration principles \\u2014 no federation primitive wrapper guide. The integation appendix is scheduled for 020H part 3, a downstream slice.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Any reader (human or agent) following the ADOPT-001 \\u00a712 pointer today finds general architecture principles, not the federation integration guide. Governance audit trail is weakened: cited conformance reference does not yet exist in the cited document at the expected content. Forward references in governance-sensitive workflow headers are a completeness gap per the no-silent-descope invariant.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Amend wrapper header to annotate the forward reference: change '(ADOPT-001 \\u00a712)' to '(ADOPT-001 \\u00a712, federation integration appendix \\u2014 pending 020H part 3 publication)'. This is a one-line comment change that closes the misleading pointer before it propagates.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-NIT-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020D1 spec text '@<commit-SHA-of-v1.0.0-rc.1>' is timing-ambiguous: the tag is cut after 020D1 merges, not before\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020D1: 'SCP's own repo adds policy-check-wrapper.yml calling the reusable workflow at @<commit-SHA-of-v1.0.0-rc.1>.' 020H part 1 (immediately downstream): 'Cut v1.0.0-rc.1 tag from the 020D1 merge commit.' The wrapper correctly pins at `9820489fa83f64d04f641d20e99d3933cefbb04a` (020K merge SHA, latest main at PR-open). Dispatch note and wrapper comments document the ratchet lifecycle (020D1 pins 020K SHA \\u2192 020H.1 cuts tag \\u2192 020D1.1 ratchets pin). The implementation is correct; the spec phrasing is ambiguous.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future reader of the 020D1 spec row could interpret '@<commit-SHA-of-v1.0.0-rc.1>' as requiring the v1.0.0-rc.1 SHA to be known at PR-open time, which is temporally impossible since the tag is cut from the merge commit. Ambiguity about the 020D1 vs 020D1.1 ratchet split.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Amend WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020D1 spec text to: 'calling the reusable workflow at @<most-recent-main-SHA at PR-open time>; pin ratcheted to v1.0.0-rc.1 SHA in follow-up slice 020D1.1.' Non-blocking for 020D1 merge.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-NIT-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"docs/reviews/** path is not covered by CODEOWNERS; governance evidence artifacts are modifiable without mandatory CODEOWNER review signal\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"CODEOWNERS covers: policies/**, schemas/**, lib/**, .github/**, tests/**, renovate/**, docs/DECISIONS.md, docs/security/**, docs/adoption/**, docs/plans/**, docs/integrations/**, output/findings/**, output/control-tower/**, output/ci/**, /CODEOWNERS, docs/CODEOWNERS. No entry for docs/reviews/**. 020K R1+R2 review closed 7 MAJ + 4 MIN findings by extending coverage; docs/reviews/ was not added. Dispatch evidence dir docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020d1/ holds R1 review JSON outputs.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"In a multi-maintainer future, review evidence artifacts (dispatch notes, R1 review JSON) could be added or modified in a PR without triggering CODEOWNER review request. In current solo-operator model this is moot. The gap will compound when a second maintainer onboards: per 020K, at that point CODEOWNERS coverage should already be comprehensive and a retroactive fix may be missed.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add `docs/reviews/** @jrnb2024` to CODEOWNERS in this PR or at the quarterly bus-factor-1 escalation review (2026-07-21). Alternatively, add an explicit exclusion note in the CODEOWNERS header explaining why docs/reviews/ is intentionally not covered. Non-blocking for 020D1.\\\"}],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Slice 020D1 is structurally sound: wrapper exists at the correct path, 40-char SHA pin `9820489fa83f64d04f641d20e99d3933cefbb04a` matches the 020K merge commit in main history, advisory-mode assertion is accurate, fork PR refusal is implemented, permissions are `contents: read, statuses: write` per D-029, no `secrets:` block, pin lifecycle is clearly documented in wrapper header and dispatch note, risk profile correctly marked HIGH with CI fixpoint categories enumerated, and the slice is a pure additive change (wrapper + dispatch evidence dir only). One MAJ finding (plan canonical template wrong repo name \\u2014 no trailing dash) is a pre-existing spec doc defect that must be resolved before 020H part 3 publication but does not block 020D1 merge. Two MIN findings (fork PR refusal AC gap in canonical example; ADOPT-001 \\u00a712 forward reference unannotated) require resolution before 020H part 3 lands. Two nit findings (spec text timing ambiguity; docs/reviews CODEOWNERS gap) are non-blocking. Conditional pass: merge proceeds; MAJ-001 and both MIN findings are tracked forward as obligations on 020H part 3 authorship.\\\"}\",\n./docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020d1/review-completeness.json:83:  \"stdout_tail\": \"`uses:` and `depName=`. Not a blocker for 020D1 merge (wrapper itself is correct); tracked-forward to 020H part 3 pre-publication.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"COMP-MIN-001\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"MIN\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Canonical minimal wrapper in WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 omits fork PR refusal `if:` condition; mandatory-vs-optional status is not documented for adopters\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md AC ite